window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"u": "guides/index.html", "a": "", "p": "Guides", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "Step-by-step guides for understanding how Flutter scrolling works."}, {"u": "guides/index.html", "a": "", "p": "Guides", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "Guides explain the what, why, and how of Flutter scrolling. Read the first six in order for the complete mental model — gestures, position, physics, activities, viewports, and slivers — then dip into the remaining guides whenever you need a specific piece. For help with implementing or calling a specific scrolling method, see the API section. To ground your understanding in complete, runnable widgets and render objects, see the Examples section."}, {"u": "guides/how-scrolling-works.html", "a": "", "p": "How Scrolling Works", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "Four small pieces passing one number around — the whole scrolling pipeline on one page."}, {"u": "guides/how-scrolling-works.html", "a": "", "p": "How Scrolling Works", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through the end-to-end architecture of a Flutter scroll view. By the end, you'll understand which class owns gestures, which class owns the scroll offset, which class enforces edge rules, and which class paints the visible window. We'll skip sliver geometry fields, lazy child garbage collection, and custom physics math for now — this is about how the pieces fit together, and only how they fit together."}, {"u": "guides/how-scrolling-works.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "How Scrolling Works", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "One Number in the Middle — Why all of Flutter scrolling reduces to reading and updating a single double called pixels. The Four Pieces — The distinct jobs of Scrollable, ScrollPosition, ScrollPhysics, and Viewport. Following One Drag Frame — What happens, call by call, when your finger moves 12 logical pixels up the screen. Following Liftoff — How a drag hands off to a ballistic simulation when your finger leaves the glass. What You Rarely Need to Touch — Which pieces you customize in everyday apps and which ones you almost never subclass."}, {"u": "guides/how-scrolling-works.html", "a": "one-number-in-the-middle", "p": "How Scrolling Works", "h": "One Number in the Middle", "k": "Guide", "x": "Scrolling in Flutter is the management of a single double named pixels: how many logical pixels of content currently sit before the leading edge of the viewport. When a vertical list starts at the top, pixels is 0.0. Dragging your finger upward by 180 pixels pushes 180 pixels of content above the top edge of the window, so pixels becomes 180.0. Every scrollbar thumb, pinned header, lazy list builder, and overscroll glow on screen is just reacting to that one number. Scrollable turns input into deltas, ScrollPosition owns pixels, ScrollPhysics governs legal movement, and Viewport paints the window and reports dimensions back."}, {"u": "guides/how-scrolling-works.html", "a": "the-four-pieces", "p": "How Scrolling Works", "h": "The Four Pieces", "k": "Guide", "x": "Every scroll view in Flutter — SingleChildScrollView, ListView, GridView, CustomScrollView, PageView — assembles the same four collaborators: Scrollable is a StatefulWidget that listens to drag gestures and pointer wheel events, wraps the tree in scrollbars and overscroll indicators from ScrollBehavior, and owns the lifecycle of the ScrollPosition. ScrollPosition (specifically ScrollPositionWithSingleContext in standard scroll views) is a Listenable object that holds pixels, the viewport size, the content bounds, and the active ScrollActivity. ScrollPhysics is an immutable strategy object that answers stateless math questions for the position: how much a user drag moves pixels, whether pixels may cross an edge, and what Simulation runs after liftoff. Viewport (and its RenderViewport render object) lays out visible slivers at the current pixels offset, clips and paints them, and reports viewportDimension and minScrollExtent / maxScrollExtent back to the ScrollPosition. Notice the narrow type in viewportBuilder: Viewport receives the ScrollPosition typed as its superclass ViewportOffset. The render layer never imports ScrollController, ScrollPhysics, or Scrollable. All RenderViewport sees is an observable pixels value and two callbacks for reporting dimensions back."}, {"u": "guides/how-scrolling-works.html", "a": "following-one-drag-frame", "p": "How Scrolling Works", "h": "Following One Drag Frame", "k": "Guide", "x": "Trace what happens when a user places a finger on a vertical ListView and moves it upward by 12.0 pixels (primaryDelta == -12.0): VerticalDragGestureRecognizer (installed by ScrollableState) wins the gesture arena and calls ScrollDragController.update with details.primaryDelta = -12.0. ScrollDragController forwards that delta to ScrollPositionWithSingleContext.applyUserOffset(-12.0). The position asks physics.applyPhysicsToUserOffset(this, -12.0). Inside normal bounds this returns -12.0 unchanged; when already overscrolled on iOS, BouncingScrollPhysics scales it down so pulling feels progressively stiffer. The position calls setPixels(pixels - (-12.0)), proposing pixels + 12.0 (dragging up increases pixels). Inside setPixels, physics.applyBoundaryConditions(this, proposedPixels) returns how much of the move is refused. If 0.0 is refused, _pixels updates, notifyListeners() fires, and a ScrollUpdateNotification bubbles up the widget tree. RenderViewport, which registered markNeedsLayout as a listener on ViewportOffset, lays out slivers at the new offset during the next frame and paints the shifted content. Aside: The sign flip in step 4 trips up everyone the first time they read the Flutter source. Pointer deltas are in screen coordinates (finger moving up is -12.0), while scroll offsets measure content scrolled past the top (+12.0). applyUserOffset bridges the two with pixels - delta."}, {"u": "guides/how-scrolling-works.html", "a": "following-liftoff", "p": "How Scrolling Works", "h": "Following Liftoff", "k": "Guide", "x": "When the finger leaves the screen with upward velocity, VerticalDragGestureRecognizer fires onEnd with a DragEndDetails. ScrollDragController hands the negated velocity (now positive pixels per second) to position.goBallistic(velocity): ScrollPositionWithSingleContext.goBallistic calls physics.createBallisticSimulation(this, velocity). On Android, ClampingScrollPhysics returns a ClampingScrollSimulation that decelerates under friction. On iOS, BouncingScrollPhysics returns a BouncingScrollSimulation that switches smoothly from friction into a return spring if it crosses maxScrollExtent. If the simulation is non-null, the position starts a BallisticScrollActivity, which ticks an unbounded AnimationController every vsync frame and feeds simulation.x(elapsedSeconds) into setPixels. When the simulation reports isDone (or hits a clamping wall), the activity calls goIdle(), switching the position back to IdleScrollActivity and dispatching ScrollEndNotification."}, {"u": "guides/how-scrolling-works.html", "a": "what-you-rarely-need-to-touch", "p": "How Scrolling Works", "h": "What You Rarely Need to Touch", "k": "Guide", "x": "Because responsibilities are split cleanly across the four classes, almost every customization touches just one seam: Read the offset or scroll programmatically — pass a ScrollController. Change snapping, friction, or edge bounce — subclass ScrollPhysics. React to scrolling from an ancestor widget — wrap in a NotificationListener for Scroll Notifications. Build custom scrolling layouts — compose slivers or write a RenderSliver (Sliver Layout). You almost never need to subclass Scrollable, ScrollPosition, or ScrollActivity yourself."}, {"u": "guides/how-scrolling-works.html", "a": "mental-model-summary", "p": "How Scrolling Works", "h": "Mental Model Summary", "k": "Guide", "x": "One source of truth: ScrollPosition.pixels is the single number that defines where the scroll view is. Input flows down into ScrollPosition: Scrollable converts drag and wheel events into calls on the position. Rules live on ScrollPhysics: ScrollPosition asks ScrollPhysics before accepting a user delta, crossing an edge, or starting a fling. Dimensions flow back from Viewport: During layout, RenderViewport tells ScrollPosition how tall the window is (viewportDimension) and where content starts and ends (minScrollExtent, maxScrollExtent). Rendering listens to ViewportOffset: Mutating pixels notifies RenderViewport (or _RenderSingleChildViewport), scheduling the next layout or paint pass. That's the whole scrolling machine from finger down to resting frame. Next, we'll look closely at ScrollPosition itself — the extents it tracks and the three very different methods that are allowed to change pixels."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-position.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Position", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "The one number that scrolling is about, the extents around it, and who is allowed to change it."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-position.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Position", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through ScrollPosition and the ScrollMetrics values it maintains. By the end, you'll understand what pixels, minScrollExtent, maxScrollExtent, and viewportDimension mean geometrically, when they become valid, and why the framework has three separate internal methods for changing pixels. We'll skip gesture recognizers, sliver layout internals, and multi-scroll controllers — this is about the position object, and only the position object."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-position.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Scroll Position", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "What ScrollPosition Actually Is — How ViewportOffset, ScrollMetrics, and ScrollPositionWithSingleContext relate. The Extents Around pixels — How minScrollExtent, maxScrollExtent, viewportDimension, and the extentBefore / extentInside / extentAfter getters fit together. Three Ways pixels Changes — The sharp contract difference between setPixels, correctPixels, and forcePixels. The Dimension Handshake — What happens during layout when the viewport reports new window or content sizes. Common Pitfalls — Reading pixels before first layout, mutating state during correctBy, and confusing atEdge with overscroll."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-position.html", "a": "what-scrollposition-actually-is", "p": "Scroll Position", "h": "What ScrollPosition Actually Is", "k": "Guide", "x": "ScrollPosition is the long-lived ChangeNotifier-style object (inheriting from ViewportOffset and mixing in ScrollMetrics) that owns the current scroll offset of a single Scrollable. In nearly every scroll view you use — ListView, GridView, CustomScrollView, SingleChildScrollView, PageView — the concrete runtime type of controller.position is ScrollPositionWithSingleContext. Only specialized widgets that coordinate two viewports at once (such as NestedScrollView) substitute their own ScrollPosition subclass."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-position.html", "a": "the-extents-around-pixels", "p": "Scroll Position", "h": "The Extents Around pixels", "k": "Guide", "x": "A ScrollPosition describes a movable window (viewportDimension) sliding along a strip of content bounded by minScrollExtent and maxScrollExtent. Suppose a vertical ListView is 300.0 pixels tall on screen (viewportDimension == 300.0), contains 960.0 pixels of total item height, and has been scrolled down by 180.0 pixels: A 300px viewport scrolled 180px into 960px of total content: maxScrollExtent is 960 - 300 = 660. Every convenience getter on ScrollMetrics derives directly from those four numbers: pixels 180.0 Current offset from the content origin minScrollExtent 0.0 Smallest in-range offset (0.0 unless slivers exist before the center key) maxScrollExtent 660.0 960.0 - 300.0 (0.0 when all content fits inside the viewport) viewportDimension 300.0 Main-axis size of the viewport extentBefore 180.0 max(pixels - minScrollExtent, 0.0) extentInside 300.0 Visible slice of in-range content inside the viewport extentAfter 480.0 max(maxScrollExtent - pixels, 0.0) outOfRange false pixels < minScrollExtent || pixels > maxScrollExtent Notice why maxScrollExtent is 660.0 and not 960.0: when pixels reaches 660.0, the bottom of the 300.0-tall viewport aligns with 660.0 + 300.0 = 960.0, the very end of the content. If all content fits inside the viewport, minScrollExtent and maxScrollExtent are both 0.0. Aside: minScrollExtent is not always 0.0. A CustomScrollView with a center key can grow slivers upward in the reverse direction, giving minScrollExtent a negative value such as -400.0. Always clamp against minScrollExtent, never against a hardcoded 0.0."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-position.html", "a": "three-ways-pixels-changes", "p": "Scroll Position", "h": "Three Ways pixels Changes", "k": "Guide", "x": "If you read scroll_position.dart, you'll find three methods that write to _pixels. Each exists for a different phase of the Flutter pipeline: setPixels(double newPixels) is the normal path called on every frame of a drag, fling, or animateTo. It asks physics.applyBoundaryConditions(this, newPixels) how much of the move is refused, updates _pixels, calls notifyListeners(), dispatches ScrollUpdateNotification (and OverscrollNotification if anything was refused), and returns any refused overscroll to the caller. correctPixels(double value) (and its relative helper correctBy(double correction)) mutates _pixels silently without calling notifyListeners(). Only viewports call correctPixels or correctBy, and only during performLayout. Firing listeners mid-layout would call markNeedsLayout on render objects currently being laid out, triggering framework assertions. forcePixels(double value) sets _pixels unconditionally — bypassing applyBoundaryConditions — and schedules listener notifications. jumpTo and mouse-wheel jumps use forcePixels when they have already decided the exact destination. Unless you are implementing a custom Viewport or a custom ScrollActivity, you never call these three methods directly. From app code, you call jumpTo or animateTo on ScrollController (or ScrollPosition), which wrap them safely."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-position.html", "a": "the-dimension-handshake", "p": "Scroll Position", "h": "The Dimension Handshake", "k": "Guide", "x": "When a ScrollPosition is first constructed inside ScrollableState, it knows its initialScrollOffset, but hasViewportDimension and hasContentDimensions are both false. Reading maxScrollExtent or viewportDimension before the first layout throws a assertion error. During performLayout, the viewport completes the handshake in order: Inside applyContentDimensions, the position asks physics.adjustPositionForNewDimensions(...) where pixels should sit given the new bounds. By default, RangeMaintainingScrollPhysics lies at the end of every physics chain: if the content shrank so your old pixels of 800.0 now exceeds a new maxScrollExtent of 500.0 (while you aren't actively dragging), it silently corrects pixels to 500.0 and returns false so RenderViewport immediately runs another layout pass with the corrected offset."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-position.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Scroll Position", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Reading controller.position.maxScrollExtent inside initState. At initState time, no layout pass has happened yet — the controller isn't even attached to a ScrollPosition, and even inside a first-frame build hasContentDimensions is still false. Wait for WidgetsBinding.instance.addPostFrameCallback or check position.hasContentDimensions before reading extents. Assuming pixels is never negative. On iOS and macOS, BouncingScrollPhysics lets pixels go negative when pulling past the top (pixels == -42.0) and above maxScrollExtent when pulling past the bottom. UI code that computes a 0.0..1.0 progress ratio from pixels / maxScrollExtent should clamp the result (and guard against maxScrollExtent == 0.0 to avoid NaN). Confusing atEdge with overscroll. position.atEdge is true only when pixels == minScrollExtent || pixels == maxScrollExtent exactly. The instant pixels bounces to -0.5, atEdge becomes false while outOfRange becomes true. Check extentAfter == 0.0 when you want to know \"at or past the trailing end.\" That's ScrollPosition end to end: one pixels value framed by minScrollExtent, maxScrollExtent, and viewportDimension, updated loudly during input/animation and corrected silently during layout. Next, we'll look at ScrollPhysics — the object the position consults before every move."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-physics.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Physics", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "How immutable physics chains control drag resistance, edge boundaries, and liftoff simulations."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-physics.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Physics", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through ScrollPhysics. By the end, you'll understand how physics instances chain together through applyTo, what each of the four core physics methods decides, and how Flutter's built-in physics classes differ. We'll skip AnimationController internals and sliver layout — this is about the rules of motion, and only the rules of motion."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-physics.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Scroll Physics", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "An Immutable Chain of Rules — Why ScrollPhysics holds no mutable state and delegates unsolved work to parent. How applyTo Builds the Chain — Why forgetting applyTo silently erases your custom physics subclass. The Four Decisions Physics Makes — Drag resistance, boundary enforcement, ballistic simulations, and resize adjustments. Built-in ScrollPhysics Classes — What ClampingScrollPhysics, BouncingScrollPhysics, NeverScrollableScrollPhysics, AlwaysScrollableScrollPhysics, and PageScrollPhysics actually override. Common Pitfalls — Missing applyTo, misunderstanding applyBoundaryConditions return values, and breaking overscroll springs."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-physics.html", "a": "an-immutable-chain-of-rules", "p": "Scroll Physics", "h": "An Immutable Chain of Rules", "k": "Guide", "x": "ScrollPhysics is a const-constructible strategy object with a single field, final ScrollPhysics? parent, and zero mutable state. Because physics objects hold no state of their own, every method receives a read-only ScrollMetrics snapshot (pixels, minScrollExtent, maxScrollExtent, viewportDimension) and returns a number or a Simulation. Each link in the chain overrides only the decisions it cares about and delegates the rest to super, which forwards to parent. A custom SnapScrollPhysics wrapped around the platform's BouncingScrollPhysics and RangeMaintainingScrollPhysics."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-physics.html", "a": "how-applyto-builds-the-chain", "p": "Scroll Physics", "h": "How applyTo Builds the Chain", "k": "Guide", "x": "When you write ListView(physics: const SnapScrollPhysics(itemExtent: 220)), ScrollableState combines your widget's physics with the ambient ScrollBehavior physics: Look closely at widget.physics!.applyTo(ambientPhysics). Your widget's physics sits at the front of the chain so its overrides run first, and ambientPhysics (for example BouncingScrollPhysics(parent: RangeMaintainingScrollPhysics())) becomes its parent fallback. Every ScrollPhysics subclass must override applyTo using buildParent(ancestor): Forgetting to override applyTo is the single most common ScrollPhysics bug. The base implementation on ScrollPhysics returns a plain ScrollPhysics(parent: buildParent(ancestor)), which strips your subclass off the chain before a single pixel ever moves."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-physics.html", "a": "the-four-decisions-physics-makes", "p": "Scroll Physics", "h": "The Four Decisions Physics Makes", "k": "Guide", "x": "Nearly all custom scroll physics come down to four methods: applyPhysicsToUserOffset(ScrollMetrics position, double offset) transforms a raw finger drag offset before it is subtracted from pixels. Returning offset gives 1:1 tracking; BouncingScrollPhysics applies progressive friction here when position.outOfRange is true so pulling past the top feels rubbery. applyBoundaryConditions(ScrollMetrics position, double value) inspects a proposed new pixels (value) and returns how many pixels of movement are refused (the overscroll). Returning 0.0 accepts the entire move; returning value - position.pixels refuses all of it. createBallisticSimulation(ScrollMetrics position, double velocity) runs when the finger lifts (or bounds change) and returns a Simulation? describing pixels over time. Returning null puts the position straight to idle. adjustPositionForNewDimensions(...) decides what pixels should be when minScrollExtent, maxScrollExtent, or viewportDimension changes during layout. The value returned by applyBoundaryConditions is subtracted from value by setPixels (actualNewPixels = value - overscroll) and reported in OverscrollNotification. If your math returns a value larger than the frame's delta (value - position.pixels) in magnitude, Flutter throws a debug assertion immediately."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-physics.html", "a": "built-in-scrollphysics-classes", "p": "Scroll Physics", "h": "Built-in ScrollPhysics Classes", "k": "Guide", "x": "Understanding the five standard subclasses saves you from writing custom physics when a composition of built-ins already does the job: ClampingScrollPhysics (Android / Linux / Windows default) — Refuses movement past minScrollExtent and maxScrollExtent in applyBoundaryConditions (feeding Android's stretch/glow indicator via the returned overscroll) and uses ClampingScrollSimulation for friction flings. BouncingScrollPhysics (iOS / macOS default) — Returns 0.0 from applyBoundaryConditions so pixels can move past the edges, attenuates user drag while overscrolled in applyPhysicsToUserOffset, and uses BouncingScrollSimulation to spring back into range. AlwaysScrollableScrollPhysics — Overrides shouldAcceptUserOffset to return true even when minScrollExtent == maxScrollExtent (for example, a short list that still needs pull-to-refresh). NeverScrollableScrollPhysics — Overrides allowUserScrolling and allowImplicitScrolling to return false, disabling touch drags and accessibility scroll actions while leaving programmatic jumpTo and animateTo working. RangeMaintainingScrollPhysics — Placed at the tail of every default chain by ScrollBehavior; keeps pixels clamped if content shrinks while the user is not overscrolling. Because chains compose with applyTo or parent:, you can combine them directly:"}, {"u": "guides/scroll-physics.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Scroll Physics", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Forgetting shouldUpdate when your physics has fields. If your ScrollPhysics subclass stores parameters (like itemExtent in Snapping Physics), override shouldUpdate(covariant MyPhysics old) to compare those fields. By default ScrollPhysics only checks runtimeType up the parent chain, so changing itemExtent on rebuild would be ignored. Returning a simulation when already at the target. In createBallisticSimulation, always check whether (target - position.pixels).abs() < tolerance.distance (or target == position.pixels) and return null. Returning a ScrollSpringSimulation whose start and end are identical wastes an animation frame before settling. Overriding createBallisticSimulation without delegating out-of-range overscroll. When position.outOfRange is true and velocity points away from the list, delegate to super.createBallisticSimulation(position, velocity) unless you specifically intend to replace the platform's edge return spring. That's ScrollPhysics end to end: an immutable parent chain built via applyTo that shapes user deltas, guards boundaries, and creates liftoff simulations. Next, we'll look at ScrollActivity — the stateful driver that actually runs drags, flings, and animateTo curves over time."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-activities.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Activities", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "The five runtime states a ScrollPosition moves through — Idle, Hold, Drag, Ballistic, and Driven."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-activities.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Activities", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through ScrollActivity and how a ScrollPosition transitions from resting, to held, to dragged, to coasting. By the end, you'll understand why activities exist separately from ScrollPhysics, what happens in each of the five built-in activities, and how momentum carries across consecutive flicks on iOS. We'll skip widget-level ScrollController APIs and sliver layout — this is about the position's state machine, and only its state machine."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-activities.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Scroll Activities", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "Why Activities Exist Alongside Physics — The clean split between stateless ScrollPhysics math and stateful per-gesture ScrollActivity drivers. The Five Built-in Activities — IdleScrollActivity, HoldScrollActivity, DragScrollActivity, BallisticScrollActivity, and DrivenScrollActivity. How Transitions Emit Notifications — Why beginActivity is where ScrollStartNotification, ScrollEndNotification, and isScrollingNotifier actually fire. Carried Momentum on Touch Down — How catching a fast fling with your thumb transfers speed into the next flick. When You Don't Need to Touch Activities — Why custom scrolling almost never subclasses ScrollActivity."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-activities.html", "a": "why-activities-exist-alongside-physics", "p": "Scroll Activities", "h": "Why Activities Exist Alongside Physics", "k": "Guide", "x": "ScrollPhysics is const and stateless — it cannot hold an AnimationController, a Completer for animateTo, or an active drag handle. ScrollActivity is the stateful counterpart owned by ScrollPosition. A ScrollPosition has exactly one active ScrollActivity at every moment, and replacing that activity via beginActivity disposes the old driver cleanly before starting the new one. A normal touch gesture walks Idle -> Hold -> Drag -> Ballistic -> Idle; programmatic animateTo runs Driven -> Ballistic -> Idle."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-activities.html", "a": "the-five-built-in-activities", "p": "Scroll Activities", "h": "The Five Built-in Activities", "k": "Guide", "x": "ScrollPositionWithSingleContext rotates among five concrete activity classes: IdleScrollActivity — The position is at rest. isScrolling is false, velocity is 0.0, and no ticker is running. Calling goIdle() enters this state. HoldScrollActivity — A pointer has touched down on the scrollable (position.hold(...)), stopping any in-flight fling, but hasn't dragged far enough to win the gesture arena yet. isScrolling is false. If the user lifts their finger without dragging (a tap), the hold cancels and calls goBallistic(0.0) so an overscrolled list can finish springing back. DragScrollActivity — Paired with a ScrollDragController once the drag gesture recognizer wins. isScrolling is true, velocity reports 0.0 (velocity is tracked by the gesture recognizer until liftoff), and pointer movements drive applyUserOffset. BallisticScrollActivity — Runs an AnimationController.unbounded driven by a Simulation from physics.createBallisticSimulation. On every tick it calls setPixels(simulation.x(elapsed)). If setPixels returns non-zero overscroll (meaning ClampingScrollPhysics hit a hard wall), the activity stops immediately and calls goIdle(). DrivenScrollActivity — Created by animateTo(to, duration: ..., curve: ...). Drives an AnimationController from the current pixels to to over duration with curve, completes the Future<void> returned by animateTo, and finishes with goBallistic(0.0) so jumping or animating out of bounds still settles cleanly."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-activities.html", "a": "how-transitions-emit-notifications", "p": "Scroll Activities", "h": "How Transitions Emit Notifications", "k": "Guide", "x": "Every state change funnels through ScrollPosition.beginActivity(ScrollActivity? newActivity). Comparing wasScrolling (on the outgoing activity) with isScrolling (on the incoming activity) is what drives both isScrollingNotifier and lifecycle notifications: !wasScrolling && isScrolling (for example, Hold → Drag or Idle → Driven) sets isScrollingNotifier.value = true and dispatches ScrollStartNotification. wasScrolling && isScrolling (for example, Drag → Ballistic at finger liftoff) keeps isScrollingNotifier.value = true — no ScrollEndNotification fires between the drag and its fling. From listeners' perspective, one continuous scroll is still underway. wasScrolling && !isScrolling (for example, Ballistic → Idle, or catching a Ballistic fling with a finger into Hold) sets isScrollingNotifier.value = false and dispatches ScrollEndNotification. That middle transition explains a subtle behavior that surprises many developers: lifting your finger at the end of a flick does not emit ScrollEndNotification until the resulting ballistic simulation comes to rest (or is interrupted)."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-activities.html", "a": "carried-momentum-on-touch-down", "p": "Scroll Activities", "h": "Carried Momentum on Touch Down", "k": "Guide", "x": "Have you ever flicked a long list on iOS, caught it mid-flight with your thumb, and flicked again to go even faster? That happens during the Ballistic → Hold transition: When BallisticScrollActivity is replaced by HoldScrollActivity, the position records previousVelocity in _heldPreviousVelocity. If the user immediately drags in the same direction and lifts off again, ScrollDragController.end asks physics.carriedMomentum(_heldPreviousVelocity) (BouncingScrollPhysics returns a fraction of that prior speed) and adds it to the new fling's launch velocity."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-activities.html", "a": "when-you-dont-need-to-touch-activities", "p": "Scroll Activities", "h": "When You Don't Need to Touch Activities", "k": "Guide", "x": "In normal Flutter development, you never instantiate or subclass ScrollActivity. Want custom deceleration, snapping, or spring constants? Override createBallisticSimulation on ScrollPhysics — BallisticScrollActivity will run whatever Simulation you return. Want custom duration/curve scrolling? Call controller.animateTo(...) — DrivenScrollActivity runs it for you. Want to know when scrolling starts and stops without a NotificationListener? Listen to controller.position.isScrollingNotifier, which flips between true and false right inside beginActivity. Only framework-level coordinators like NestedScrollView write custom ScrollActivity subclasses because they split a single ballistic simulation across an outer header position and an inner body position simultaneously."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-activities.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Scroll Activities", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Assuming animateTo's Future waits if a user grabs the list. Actually, DrivenScrollActivity.done does complete when interrupted by a user touch (inside dispose()), even though the destination offset to was never reached. Never assume controller.offset == targetOffset just because await controller.animateTo(targetOffset, ...) returned — check controller.offset afterward if exact arrival matters. Expecting ScrollEndNotification on finger up. Lifting your finger transitions from DragScrollActivity (isScrolling == true) to BallisticScrollActivity (isScrolling == true). ScrollEndNotification only fires when BallisticScrollActivity finishes (or if createBallisticSimulation returns null, transitioning straight to IdleScrollActivity). That's ScrollActivity end to end: one active driver at a time on ScrollPosition, transitioning cleanly via beginActivity from Idle through Hold, Drag, Ballistic, and Driven. Next, we'll step up to the widget API you hold in your own state classes: ScrollController and PrimaryScrollController."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-controllers.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Controllers", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "How ScrollController creates, observes, and commands a ScrollPosition — and what PrimaryScrollController does when you omit one."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-controllers.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Controllers", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through ScrollController, PageController, and PrimaryScrollController. By the end, you'll understand what a controller actually owns, how attach and detach work when widgets rebuild, and when Flutter automatically shares an inherited PrimaryScrollController. We'll skip ScrollNotification bubbling and sliver internals — this is about the handle you hold in widget state, and only that handle."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-controllers.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Scroll Controllers", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "A Remote Control, Not the Position — Why ScrollController delegates almost everything to its attached ScrollPositions. Lifecycle: createScrollPosition, attach, and detach — What happens when a Scrollable mounts, updates, and unmounts. Reading and Moving the Offset — offset, hasClients, jumpTo, animateTo, and listening for changes. How PrimaryScrollController Works — Platform-specific auto-inheritance, status-bar tap-to-top on iOS, and avoiding multi-attachment crashes. Page Storage and keepScrollOffset — How scroll offsets survive tab switches and route pops. Common Pitfalls — Sharing one controller across two ListViews, forgetting dispose, and reading offset when unattached."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-controllers.html", "a": "a-remote-control-not-the-position", "p": "Scroll Controllers", "h": "A Remote Control, Not the Position", "k": "Guide", "x": "ScrollController does not store pixels itself — it holds a list of zero or more attached ScrollPosition objects (positions) and forwards your reads and commands to them. Even when you omit controller: on a ListView, there is still a ScrollController at work: ScrollableState creates a private fallback ScrollController internally so every Scrollable follows the exact same code path."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-controllers.html", "a": "lifecycle-createscrollposition-attach-and-detach", "p": "Scroll Controllers", "h": "Lifecycle: createScrollPosition, attach, and detach", "k": "Guide", "x": "When ScrollableState mounts (or swaps controllers in didUpdateWidget), it runs a three-step handshake with the effective ScrollController: Calls controller.createScrollPosition(physics, context, oldPosition) to build the ScrollPosition (passing the previous ScrollPosition during rebuilds so the current pixels value carries over). Calls controller.attach(position), which adds position to controller.positions and subscribes notifyListeners to the position. On unmount or controller swap, calls controller.detach(position) before disposing the position. Subclasses of ScrollController exist primarily to customize step 1. PageController, for example, overrides createScrollPosition to return a _PagePosition that speaks in page indices alongside raw pixels."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-controllers.html", "a": "reading-and-moving-the-offset", "p": "Scroll Controllers", "h": "Reading and Moving the Offset", "k": "Guide", "x": "Once a Scrollable is mounted, controller.hasClients becomes true: controller.position and controller.offset assert that exactly one ScrollPosition is attached. If zero scrollables are attached — or if two ListViews accidentally share the same controller — reading .position or .offset throws immediately. By contrast, jumpTo and animateTo loop over every entry in  List.of(_positions), broadcasting the move to all attached positions. When you subscribe with controller.addListener(...), your callback fires whenever pixels changes — not when maxScrollExtent changes without moving pixels. If you need to react to content-size changes too, listen for ScrollMetricsNotification instead (covered in Scroll Notifications)."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-controllers.html", "a": "how-primaryscrollcontroller-works", "p": "Scroll Controllers", "h": "How PrimaryScrollController Works", "k": "Guide", "x": "PrimaryScrollController is an InheritedWidget placed near the top of Scaffold / CupertinoPageScaffold so ancestor chrome can find the screen's main vertical scroll view without manual plumbing. On iOS, tapping the system status bar animates the PrimaryScrollController back to 0.0; on desktop, keyboard shortcuts (Page Up, Page Down, Space) can target the primary scrollable. A ScrollView (ListView, GridView, CustomScrollView) automatically adopts PrimaryScrollController.maybeOf(context) only when all of these hold: You left both controller: null and primary: null (or explicitly passed primary: true). scrollDirection is Axis.vertical. PrimaryScrollController.shouldInherit(context, scrollDirection) returns true — which by default only happens on mobile platforms (TargetPlatform.iOS, android, fuchsia). Once a ScrollView claims the primary controller, it wraps its subtree in PrimaryScrollController.none so nested vertical lists inside it don't collide on the same controller."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-controllers.html", "a": "page-storage-and-keepscrolloffset", "p": "Scroll Controllers", "h": "Page Storage and keepScrollOffset", "k": "Guide", "x": "By default, ScrollController.keepScrollOffset is true. Whenever an activity settles (and during scroll updates), ScrollPosition writes pixels into the nearest PageStorage bucket keyed by the Scrollable's PageStorageKey. Giving two tabs distinct PageStorageKeys lets TabBarView unmount an off-screen tab and restore its exact scroll offset when the user swipes back — even without keeping a ScrollController alive in parent state."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-controllers.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Scroll Controllers", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Two sibling ListViews accidentally sharing PrimaryScrollController. If you put two vertical ListViews side by side on iOS (for example in a tablet split-view) with neither controller: nor primary: false, both try to attach to the scaffold's single PrimaryScrollController, throwing \"ScrollController attached to multiple scroll views\". Pass primary: false (or explicit controllers) to secondary lists. Forgetting dispose() on a ScrollController. A ScrollController is a ChangeNotifier. Leaving it undisposed leaks listener closures and triggers memory-leak tracker failures in flutter_test. Calling jumpTo before hasClients is true. If a ListView sits behind a loading spinner and hasn't mounted yet, _controller.jumpTo(0) does nothing (its _positions list is empty), while _controller.offset throws. Always guard with if (_controller.hasClients). That's ScrollController end to end: a lightweight handle that creates, attaches to, and drives ScrollPositions while PrimaryScrollController wires the main vertical list to platform chrome. Next, we'll look at how scrolling events travel up the widget tree via ScrollNotification."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-notifications.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Notifications", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "How scroll events bubble up the element tree, how depth tracks nested viewports, and why ScrollMetricsNotification is separate."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-notifications.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Notifications", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through ScrollNotification, NotificationListener, and ScrollMetricsNotification. By the end, you'll understand every notification type a scroll view emits, how notification.depth isolates nested scrollables, and when to prefer notifications over a ScrollController listener. We'll skip gesture arena mechanics and sliver geometry — this is about observing scroll events from above, and only that."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-notifications.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Scroll Notifications", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "Why Notifications Exist Alongside Controllers — Observing any descendant scrollable without passing a ScrollController down through ten widgets. The Five ScrollNotification Subclasses — Start, update, overscroll, end, and user-direction notifications. Nested Scroll Views and depth — How ViewportElementMixin increments depth so outer widgets ignore inner carousels. ScrollMetricsNotification When Content Resizes — Catching layout-driven extent changes that never move pixels. Common Pitfalls — Missing depth == 0, returning true by mistake, and calling setState on every pixel."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-notifications.html", "a": "why-notifications-exist-alongside-controllers", "p": "Scroll Notifications", "h": "Why Notifications Exist Alongside Controllers", "k": "Guide", "x": "A ScrollController works top-down: a parent creates it and hands it explicitly to one specific ScrollView. That breaks down as soon as a reusable wrapper widget — like RefreshIndicator, Scrollbar, or a collapsing app-bar shell — needs to observe whatever scrollable the caller placed inside its child subtree. ScrollNotification works bottom-up. Whenever a ScrollPosition starts, moves, overscrolls, or stops, it dispatches a notification from its Scrollable's BuildContext that bubbles up every ancestor Element until it hits the root or a NotificationListener returns true. Return false from onNotification unless you intentionally want to swallow the event. Returning true stops bubbling, which will freeze any ancestor Scrollbar or RefreshIndicator waiting on the same stream."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-notifications.html", "a": "the-five-scrollnotification-subclasses", "p": "Scroll Notifications", "h": "The Five ScrollNotification Subclasses", "k": "Guide", "x": "Every ScrollNotification carries an immutable ScrollMetrics snapshot (notification.metrics) and the originating BuildContext. Switching on the concrete subtype tells you what happened: ScrollStartNotification Activity flips from not-scrolling to scrolling dragDetails (non-null if started by touch) ScrollUpdateNotification pixels changed during layout or frame tick scrollDelta, dragDetails OverscrollNotification applyBoundaryConditions refused part of a move overscroll, velocity, dragDetails ScrollEndNotification Activity flips back to idle / hold dragDetails (non-null if ended right on finger up) UserScrollNotification User drag direction changes (forward, reverse, idle) direction (ScrollDirection) Aside: In UserScrollNotification, ScrollDirection.reverse means scrolling down toward higher pixels (finger moving up), while ScrollDirection.forward means scrolling back toward 0.0. The enum names describe the movement of the content relative to its origin, which feels inverted until you remember that content moves -pixels."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-notifications.html", "a": "nested-scroll-views-and-depth", "p": "Scroll Notifications", "h": "Nested Scroll Views and depth", "k": "Guide", "x": "Suppose a vertical ListView contains a horizontal PageView carousel on row 0. When the user swipes the carousel horizontally, its ScrollPosition dispatches ScrollUpdateNotifications that bubble right through your outer NotificationListener! Flutter solves this with ViewportNotificationMixin.depth. Every viewport element mixes in ViewportElementMixin, which increments notification.depth by 1 as a notification passes out of that viewport: A notification from the outer ListView passes through zero enclosing viewports before reaching a listener right above the ListView, so notification.depth == 0. A notification from the inner PageView passes through the PageView's own viewport and the outer ListView's viewport, arriving with notification.depth == 1. Checking notification.depth == 0 (or notification.metrics.axis == Axis.vertical) keeps inner carousels from triggering outer pull-to-refresh indicators or pagination loaders."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-notifications.html", "a": "scrollmetricsnotification-when-content-resizes", "p": "Scroll Notifications", "h": "ScrollMetricsNotification When Content Resizes", "k": "Guide", "x": "What if your list sits at pixels == 0.0, new items stream in so maxScrollExtent grows from 0.0 to 800.0, and the user hasn't touched the screen? No ScrollUpdateNotification fires because pixels didn't change — yet a \"Scroll to bottom\" button or custom scrollbar thumb now needs to appear. For that exact case, ScrollPosition.applyContentDimensions schedules a ScrollMetricsNotification (via microtask after layout) whenever the metrics snapshot changes: Notice that ScrollMetricsNotification extends Notification directly, not ScrollNotification. A NotificationListener<ScrollNotification> will never receive it — listen for ScrollMetricsNotification explicitly when you care about resize events."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-notifications.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Scroll Notifications", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Calling setState unconditionally inside onNotification. ScrollUpdateNotification fires at 60Hz–120Hz. Calling setState on every notification rebuilds your entire subtree every frame; compute a boolean threshold (such as final bool scrolled = notification.metrics.pixels > 0) and only call setState when that boolean flips. Triggering infinite load-more loops near the bottom. Checking notification.metrics.extentAfter < 200 inside ScrollUpdateNotification will fire dozens of times while the user scrolls through the final 200 pixels. Guard your pagination call with an _isLoading flag and check notification.depth == 0. That's ScrollNotification end to end: five bubbling lifecycle events plus out-of-band ScrollMetricsNotification, scoped cleanly across nested viewports by depth. Next, we'll look at ScrollBehavior and ScrollConfiguration — how Flutter decides platform physics, scrollbars,overscroll indicators, and allowed drag devices across an entire subtree."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-behavior.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Behavior", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "How ScrollConfiguration and ScrollBehavior supply default physics, scrollbars, overscroll indicators, and drag devices."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-behavior.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Behavior", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through ScrollBehavior and ScrollConfiguration. By the end, you'll understand where default iOS bounce vs Android stretch comes from, why desktop mouse dragging is disabled by default (and how to enable it in three lines), and how to swap scrollbars or overscroll effects across one screen or your whole app. We'll skip custom ScrollPhysics math and sliver layout — this is about ambient scrolling policy, and only ambient policy."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-behavior.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Scroll Behavior", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "ScrollBehavior vs ScrollConfiguration — The policy object and the InheritedWidget that distributes it. The Four Things ScrollBehavior Controls — Default physics, scrollbars, overscroll indicators, and dragDevices. copyWith vs Subclassing — Changing behavior for one subtree without writing a new class. Enabling Mouse and Trackpad Dragging — Why PointerDeviceKind.mouse is excluded by default and how to opt in. Common Pitfalls — Overwriting MaterialScrollBehavior accidentally and stacking duplicate Scrollbars."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-behavior.html", "a": "scrollbehavior-vs-scrollconfiguration", "p": "Scroll Behavior", "h": "ScrollBehavior vs ScrollConfiguration", "k": "Guide", "x": "Whenever a Scrollable builds without an explicit physics: argument, where does it get BouncingScrollPhysics on iOS and ClampingScrollPhysics on Android? It reads ScrollConfiguration.of(context), an InheritedWidget that holds a ScrollBehavior instance: MaterialApp installs MaterialScrollBehavior at the root of your app (unless you pass MaterialApp(scrollBehavior: ...)). CupertinoApp installs CupertinoScrollBehavior. Bare WidgetsApp (or tests using Directionality alone) falls back to the base ScrollBehavior."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-behavior.html", "a": "the-four-things-scrollbehavior-controls", "p": "Scroll Behavior", "h": "The Four Things ScrollBehavior Controls", "k": "Guide", "x": "During ScrollableState.build and _updatePosition, Scrollable asks the ambient ScrollBehavior four questions: getScrollPhysics(BuildContext context) — Returns the ambient ScrollPhysics chain. On iOS and macOS, base ScrollBehavior returns BouncingScrollPhysics(parent: RangeMaintainingScrollPhysics()); on android, fuchsia, linux, and windows, it returns ClampingScrollPhysics(parent: RangeMaintainingScrollPhysics()). buildScrollbar(context, child, details) — Wraps the viewport in a platform scrollbar on desktop platforms (macOS, linux, windows). MaterialScrollBehavior wraps with Scrollbar; CupertinoScrollBehavior wraps with CupertinoScrollbar. On mobile touch platforms, it returns child untouched unless you add a Scrollbar widget yourself. buildOverscrollIndicator(context, child, details) — Wraps the viewport in an edge effect when clamping physics refuse movement. MaterialScrollBehavior wraps Android views in StretchingOverscrollIndicator (under Material 3) or GlowingOverscrollIndicator (Material 2); iOS needs none because BouncingScrollPhysics moves the content itself. dragDevices — Returns the Set<PointerDeviceKind> allowed to start drag gestures (touch, stylus, invertedStylus, trackpad, and unknown by default — not mouse)."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-behavior.html", "a": "copywith-vs-subclassing", "p": "Scroll Behavior", "h": "copyWith vs Subclassing", "k": "Guide", "x": "For local tweaks — removing scrollbars around a custom carousel, turning off the Android stretch effect on one screen, or changing physics for a subtree — call ScrollConfiguration.of(context).copyWith(...) instead of writing a subclass: For app-wide policy, pass a custom ScrollBehavior to MaterialApp.scrollBehavior: Extend or copyWith MaterialScrollBehavior (not base ScrollBehavior) inside a Material app. Extending raw ScrollBehavior drops Material's desktop Scrollbar and Android's StretchingOverscrollIndicator, reverting Android edges to an old cyan glow or nothing at all."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-behavior.html", "a": "enabling-mouse-and-trackpad-dragging", "p": "Scroll Behavior", "h": "Enabling Mouse and Trackpad Dragging", "k": "Guide", "x": "On web and desktop, Flutter intentionally excludes PointerDeviceKind.mouse from ScrollBehavior.dragDevices: users expect clicking and dragging with a mouse to select text or drag items, while scrolling happens via mouse wheel, trackpad two-finger pan (PointerDeviceKind.trackpad), or dragging the scrollbar thumb. For horizontal card carousels or canvas-like surfaces where mouse click-drag should scroll, add PointerDeviceKind.mouse via copyWith:"}, {"u": "guides/scroll-behavior.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Scroll Behavior", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Wrapping a desktop ListView in Scrollbar without a shared ScrollController. On macOS, Windows, and Linux, MaterialScrollBehavior already wraps every vertical scroll view in an automatic Scrollbar. Adding a manual Scrollbar(child: ListView(...)) on desktop draws two scrollbars — or triggers a Scrollbar position assertion unless you either pass the same controller to both or set scrollbars: false on ScrollConfiguration. Passing physics: on a ListView expecting it to replace ScrollBehavior completely. Remember from Scroll Physics that widget physics prepend onto ScrollBehavior.getScrollPhysics(context) via applyTo. If you pass AlwaysScrollableScrollPhysics() on a ListView, its parent link is still the ambient BouncingScrollPhysics or ClampingScrollPhysics from ScrollBehavior. That finishes the widget-and-position half of Flutter scrolling. Next, we cross into the rendering half — starting with Viewport and how a single-child scroll view differs fundamentally from a sliver viewport."}, {"u": "guides/viewports.html", "a": "", "p": "Viewports", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "What a viewport actually does, why SingleChildScrollView only repaints while Viewport relayouts, and what shrinkWrap costs."}, {"u": "guides/viewports.html", "a": "", "p": "Viewports", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through Flutter's viewport render objects: _RenderSingleChildViewport, RenderViewport, and RenderShrinkWrappingViewport. By the end, you'll understand why SingleChildScrollView and CustomScrollView take completely different approaches to scrolling, how RenderViewport lays out slivers from an anchor or center key, and why shrinkWrap: true changes layout complexity from $O(\\text{visible})$ to $O(\\text{all items})$. We'll skip individual SliverGeometry fields until the next guide — this is about the window itself, and only the window."}, {"u": "guides/viewports.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Viewports", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "The Viewport's Three Jobs — Sizing the window, reporting extents to ViewportOffset, and painting clipped content. Box Viewport vs Sliver Viewport — Why SingleChildScrollView calls markNeedsPaint on scroll while RenderViewport calls markNeedsLayout. Layout Retries and Offset Corrections — How RenderViewport.performLayout handles slivers whose estimated scroll extents shift mid-scroll. center and anchor — Growing scroll content in both directions from a middle item (like a chat timeline). What shrinkWrap: true Really Does — Replacing Viewport with ShrinkWrappingViewport and what it costs. Common Pitfalls — Tall columns inside SingleChildScrollView, unbounded viewport constraints, and nested shrinkWrap lists."}, {"u": "guides/viewports.html", "a": "the-viewports-three-jobs", "p": "Viewports", "h": "The Viewport's Three Jobs", "k": "Guide", "x": "No matter how simple or sophisticated a viewport is, its contract with the rest of Flutter comes down to three responsibilities: Choose a RenderBox size inside the incoming BoxConstraints from its parent widget, and pass the main-axis length to offset.applyViewportDimension(...). Measure scrollable bounds (minScrollExtent and maxScrollExtent) and pass them to offset.applyContentDimensions(...). Paint visible children shifted by -offset.pixels inside a clip rectangle (when clipBehavior != Clip.none), applying the same shift to hit-testing and coordinate transforms. You can see all three jobs in roughly 100 lines of plain RenderBox code in the Minimal Viewport example."}, {"u": "guides/viewports.html", "a": "box-viewport-vs-sliver-viewport", "p": "Viewports", "h": "Box Viewport vs Sliver Viewport", "k": "Guide", "x": "Flutter ships two fundamentally different viewport architectures, and choosing the wrong one is behind most scrolling performance bugs: Child protocol One RenderBox Zero or more RenderSlivers Child constraints Unbounded (double.infinity) along scroll axis SliverConstraints with scrollOffset & remainingPaintExtent Listener on ViewportOffset markNeedsPaint markNeedsLayout Off-screen widgets Built, laid out, and kept in memory all at once Built and laid out lazily only inside visible + cache window Look at row 3: SingleChildScrollView lays out its entire child once up front, then only repaints on scroll; RenderViewport runs sliver layout on every scroll frame. Running layout on every scroll frame sounds expensive until you realize only the slivers overlapping the viewport (plus cacheExtent) lay out any box children. Scrolling a 10,000-row ListView.builder touches ~15 rows per frame; putting those 10,000 rows in a SingleChildScrollView(child: Column(...)) builds and lays out all 10,000 on frame 1."}, {"u": "guides/viewports.html", "a": "layout-retries-and-offset-corrections", "p": "Viewports", "h": "Layout Retries and Offset Corrections", "k": "Guide", "x": "When you scroll backward through a variable-height ListView.builder, an item re-entering the top of the viewport might turn out to be 140px tall when the list previously assumed it started 80px earlier. If the viewport didn't adjust pixels, visible items on screen would suddenly jump downward by 60px. RenderViewport.performLayout handles this with a retry loop (up to 10 * childCount attempts per frame): Whenever a sliver reports a non-zero geometry.scrollOffsetCorrection — or applyContentDimensions adjusts pixels because the content shrank — RenderViewport corrects offset silently and re-runs _attemptLayout within the same layout pass so no broken intermediate frame ever reaches the screen."}, {"u": "guides/viewports.html", "a": "center-and-anchor", "p": "Viewports", "h": "center and anchor", "k": "Guide", "x": "Normally a Viewport lays out all its slivers in the forward direction starting from pixels == 0.0. Two properties on CustomScrollView / Viewport unlock bidirectional and anchored layouts: center (Key?) points to one child sliver as the 0.0 origin. Slivers after center lay out forward (pixels >= 0); slivers before center lay out in reverse (pixels < 0, producing a negative minScrollExtent). Chat apps use center so prepending older messages above the origin never shifts pixels for newer messages below it. anchor (0.0 to 1.0, default 0.0) shifts where 0.0 sits inside the viewport window. anchor: 0.5 places the center sliver's leading edge dead-center in the viewport instead of flush against the top edge."}, {"u": "guides/viewports.html", "a": "what-shrinkwrap-true-really-does", "p": "Viewports", "h": "What shrinkWrap: true Really Does", "k": "Guide", "x": "Normally RenderViewport sizes itself to constraints.biggest — it fills the screen height handed down by its parent without asking slivers how tall all their items are. When you set shrinkWrap: true on a ListView or CustomScrollView, Flutter swaps Viewport out for ShrinkWrappingViewport (RenderShrinkWrappingViewport), which must size itself to the sum of its slivers' content (up to constraints.maxHeight): To know its own height, RenderShrinkWrappingViewport passes remainingPaintExtent: double.infinity (when unbounded) and walks slivers until their extents are known. Lazy builders lose virtualization when shrink-wrapped inside unbounded constraints. Putting ListView.builder(shrinkWrap: true, physics: NeverScrollableScrollPhysics(), ...) inside another ListView forces Flutter to build every single item in the inner list on the first frame. Replace that inner ListView with a SliverList inside one shared CustomScrollView instead."}, {"u": "guides/viewports.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Viewports", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Putting a ListView inside a Column without Expanded. Column passes maxHeight: double.infinity to its children, and RenderViewport tries to fill constraints.biggest — throwing \"Vertical viewport was given unbounded height\". Wrap the ListView in Expanded or Flexible so it receives a finite height. Using IntrinsicHeight inside or around a Viewport. Slivers depend on a scroll offset to decide which children exist, so RenderViewport cannot compute intrinsic dimensions and throws if an ancestor asks for getMinIntrinsicHeight / getMaxIntrinsicHeight. That's Viewport end to end: SingleChildScrollView repaints one pre-laid-out RenderBox, RenderViewport lays out slivers lazily around pixels (with mid-layout correction retries), and ShrinkWrappingViewport trades lazy sizing for content-hugging bounds. Next, we'll open up the sliver layout protocol itself: SliverConstraints in, SliverGeometry out."}, {"u": "guides/sliver-layout.html", "a": "", "p": "Sliver Layout", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "Why RenderSliver exists, how SliverConstraints go in, and how SliverGeometry comes back out."}, {"u": "guides/sliver-layout.html", "a": "", "p": "Sliver Layout", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through the sliver layout protocol (RenderSliver, SliverConstraints, and SliverGeometry). By the end, you'll understand why BoxConstraints cannot express lazy scrolling, what the viewport hands each sliver during layout, and how the three extents on SliverGeometry (scrollExtent, paintExtent, and layoutExtent) control everything on screen. We'll skip multi-child garbage collection and app-bar widgets for now — this is about the sliver layout handshake, and only that handshake."}, {"u": "guides/sliver-layout.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Sliver Layout", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "Why BoxConstraints Aren't Enough — What information a lazy list needs that minWidth..maxHeight cannot carry. What SliverConstraints Tell a Sliver — scrollOffset, remainingPaintExtent, overlap, and crossAxisExtent. What SliverGeometry Answers Back — Distinguishing scrollExtent, paintExtent, and layoutExtent with concrete numbers. How RenderViewport Walks Slivers — How one sliver's SliverGeometry shapes the next sliver's SliverConstraints. Geometry Invariants — The exact rules SliverGeometry.debugAssertIsValid enforces in debug mode. Common Pitfalls — Confusing scrollOffset with pixels, exceeding remainingPaintExtent, and forgetting asBoxConstraints."}, {"u": "guides/sliver-layout.html", "a": "why-boxconstraints-arent-enough", "p": "Sliver Layout", "h": "Why BoxConstraints Aren't Enough", "k": "Guide", "x": "A RenderBox receives four numbers (minWidth, maxWidth, minHeight, maxHeight) and returns a Size(width, height). Nowhere in BoxConstraints can a parent say: \"The user has scrolled 420 pixels past your top edge, and only 300 pixels of screen height remain below you — only build and lay out the slice that shows.\" RenderSliver replaces BoxConstraints → Size with SliverConstraints → SliverGeometry. Instead of laying out a fixed rectangle, a sliver lays out a slice of scrollable space. RenderViewport passes SliverConstraints down to each RenderSliver, which assigns geometry before performLayout returns."}, {"u": "guides/sliver-layout.html", "a": "what-sliverconstraints-tell-a-sliver", "p": "Sliver Layout", "h": "What SliverConstraints Tell a Sliver", "k": "Guide", "x": "Inside RenderSliver.performLayout(), this.constraints gives you a SliverConstraints snapshot tailored to this specific sliver's position in the scroll view: scrollOffset (>= 0.0) — How many pixels of this sliver have already scrolled past the viewport's leading edge. While earlier slivers haven't finished scrolling off yet, your scrollOffset is 0.0. remainingPaintExtent (>= 0.0) — How many pixels of visible viewport space remain from this sliver's layout start to the trailing edge of the viewport. overlap — How many pixels at the start of this sliver's space are currently painted over by an earlier pinned or floating sliver (such as PinnedHeaderSliver). crossAxisExtent — The width of a vertical viewport (or height of a horizontal one). Calling constraints.asBoxConstraints() turns this directly into tight cross-axis BoxConstraints for a child RenderBox. precedingScrollExtent — Total scrollExtent of all slivers before this one."}, {"u": "guides/sliver-layout.html", "a": "what-slivergeometry-answers-back", "p": "Sliver Layout", "h": "What SliverGeometry Answers Back", "k": "Guide", "x": "A RenderSliver has no size getter. Instead, performLayout must assign geometry = SliverGeometry(...) before returning. Three fields do almost all the work: scrollExtent — Total scrollable length this sliver contributes to maxScrollExtent, whether currently visible or not. paintExtent — How many pixels this sliver actually paints inside the viewport right now (must never exceed remainingPaintExtent). layoutExtent — How far RenderViewport should advance the starting position for the next sliver (defaults to paintExtent when omitted). Walk through a concrete 200px SliverToBoxAdapter inside a 500px viewport as the user scrolls: At scrollOffset == 0.0: All 200px are on screen. scrollExtent: 200, paintExtent: 200, layoutExtent: 200. The next sliver starts at y = 200. At scrollOffset == 80.0: Top 80px have scrolled off; 120px remain visible. scrollExtent: 200, paintExtent: 120, layoutExtent: 120. The next sliver starts at y = 120. At scrollOffset == 250.0: All 200px have scrolled past the top. scrollExtent: 200, paintExtent: 0, layoutExtent: 0 (SliverGeometry.zero except scrollExtent: 200). The next sliver starts at y = 0 with scrollOffset = 250 - 200 = 50.0! RenderSliver provides calculatePaintOffset and calculateCacheOffset as built-in helper methods so you don't have to hand-roll the clamping arithmetic between [from, to] and [scrollOffset, scrollOffset + remainingPaintExtent]."}, {"u": "guides/sliver-layout.html", "a": "how-renderviewport-walks-slivers", "p": "Sliver Layout", "h": "How RenderViewport Walks Slivers", "k": "Guide", "x": "Seeing how RenderViewport updates its running totals after each child makes every field obvious: Before laying out sliver $i$, the viewport computes sliverScrollOffset = max(0.0, totalScrollOffset - precedingScrollExtent). Sliver $i$ runs performLayout() and writes geometry. The viewport adds geometry.scrollExtent to precedingScrollExtent. The viewport advances the paint cursor by geometry.layoutExtent and subtracts geometry.layoutExtent from remainingPaintExtent for sliver $i + 1$. If geometry.paintExtent > geometry.layoutExtent (as in a Sticky Header), the extra painted height becomes overlap for sliver $i + 1$."}, {"u": "guides/sliver-layout.html", "a": "geometry-invariants", "p": "Sliver Layout", "h": "Geometry Invariants", "k": "Guide", "x": "In debug mode, SliverGeometry asserts a handful of strict inequalities. Violating any of them throws a detailed FlutterError during layout: paintExtent <= remainingPaintExtent (specifically paintOrigin + paintExtent <= remainingPaintExtent) — A sliver may never claim to paint past the end of the viewport. layoutExtent <= paintExtent — You cannot push the next sliver further down than the space you actually paint. maxPaintExtent >= paintExtent — Your maximum possible paint size must be at least what you are painting right now."}, {"u": "guides/sliver-layout.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Sliver Layout", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Treating constraints.scrollOffset as ScrollPosition.pixels. They are equal only for the very first sliver in the viewport. If 300px of slivers precede your sliver and pixels == 320.0, your constraints.scrollOffset is 20.0, not 320.0. If you truly need the global scroll offset, add constraints.precedingScrollExtent + constraints.scrollOffset (keeping in mind scrollOffset is clamped at 0.0 until scroll reaches your sliver). Forgetting floating-point epsilon when clamping paintExtent. Subtraction like childExtent - constraints.scrollOffset can occasionally exceed constraints.remainingPaintExtent by 1e-13, tripping debug assertions. Always clamp against constraints.remainingPaintExtent (or use calculatePaintOffset, which clamps for you). That's the sliver layout protocol end to end: SliverConstraints tell each sliver how far past its start the user has scrolled and how much screen remains, and SliverGeometry reports total scrollExtent, visible paintExtent, and advancing layoutExtent. Next, we'll see how SliverList and SliverGrid use this protocol to build and garbage-collect thousands of box children lazily."}, {"u": "guides/lazy-children.html", "a": "", "p": "Lazy Children", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "How SliverList and SliverGrid build widgets mid-layout, manage the cacheExtent window, and honor AutomaticKeepAlive."}, {"u": "guides/lazy-children.html", "a": "", "p": "Lazy Children", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through lazy child virtualization in SliverList, SliverGrid, SliverChildDelegate, and RenderSliverMultiBoxAdaptor. By the end, you'll understand how a render object triggers widget builds during performLayout, how cacheExtent decides when off-screen items mount and unmount, why itemExtent / prototypeItem transform jump performance, and how AutomaticKeepAliveClientMixin parks elements off-screen without destroying their State. We'll skip custom 2D viewports and sticky headers — this is about 1D lazy child management, and only that."}, {"u": "guides/lazy-children.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Lazy Children", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "Three Cooperating Layers — How SliverChildDelegate, SliverMultiBoxAdaptorElement, and RenderSliverMultiBoxAdaptor divide lazy building. Building Widgets Mid-Layout — How invokeLayoutCallback lets RenderSliverList ask its Element for item index + 1 on the fly. cacheExtent and Garbage Collection — When leading and trailing children are built and when collectGarbage unmounts them. Why itemExtent and prototypeItem Matter — Avoiding $O(N)$ layout walks on long jumpTo / scrollbar drags. Keeping Off-Screen State Alive — How AutomaticKeepAliveClientMixin moves unmounted items into _keepAliveBucket. Common Pitfalls — addAutomaticKeepAlives memory leaks, missing keys on mutable lists, and expensive itemBuilder work."}, {"u": "guides/lazy-children.html", "a": "three-cooperating-layers", "p": "Lazy Children", "h": "Three Cooperating Layers", "k": "Guide", "x": "Normally Flutter builds widgets first (build phase) and lays out render objects second (layout phase). A lazy ListView.builder with 10,000 variable-height items cannot work in that order: during build, nobody knows whether 3 items or 14 items will fit inside a 600px viewport! Flutter solves this by splitting SliverList across three objects: SliverChildDelegate (SliverChildBuilderDelegate or SliverChildListDelegate) — Knows how to turn an int index into a Widget? (returning null past the end). SliverMultiBoxAdaptorElement (implements RenderSliverBoxChildManager) — Holds a SplayTreeMap<int, Element> of currently mounted item elements and builds or unmounts them on demand. RenderSliverMultiBoxAdaptor (base class of RenderSliverList, RenderSliverFixedExtentList, and RenderSliverGrid) — Maintains a doubly linked list of active RenderBox children and walks forward/backward until the visible + cache window is covered."}, {"u": "guides/lazy-children.html", "a": "building-widgets-mid-layout", "p": "Lazy Children", "h": "Building Widgets Mid-Layout", "k": "Guide", "x": "Inside RenderSliverList.performLayout, each step forward measures the current child's bottom edge. If space remains inside scrollOffset + remainingCacheExtent, the render object asks its childManager (the Element) to materialize index + 1 right then and there via invokeLayoutCallback: Because itemBuilder runs inside the sliver's layout pass, an itemBuilder cannot mutate widget state above the viewport. Calling setState on an ancestor while itemBuilder executes will throw \"setState() or markNeedsBuild() called during build/layout\"."}, {"u": "guides/lazy-children.html", "a": "cacheextent-and-garbage-collection", "p": "Lazy Children", "h": "cacheExtent and Garbage Collection", "k": "Guide", "x": "If RenderSliverList only kept strictly visible items alive, scrolling 1px would constantly build a row at one edge while destroying one at the other, and images one pixel off-screen wouldn't start decoding until already in view. Viewport extends the active window in both directions by a cache margin (250.0 logical pixels by default, configurable via scrollCacheExtent: ScrollCacheExtent.pixels(...) or .viewport(...)): RenderSliverList keeps items mounted across the visible viewport plus leading and trailing cache regions, and unmounts everything outside. At the end of performLayout, RenderSliverList counts how many mounted children now lie completely before cacheOrigin (leadingGarbage) and completely after the trailing cache edge (trailingGarbage), and calls: collectGarbage tells SliverMultiBoxAdaptorElement to unmount those off-cache Elements (disposing their State and RenderBox), keeping memory proportional to screen height rather than list length."}, {"u": "guides/lazy-children.html", "a": "why-itemextent-and-prototypeitem-matter", "p": "Lazy Children", "h": "Why itemExtent and prototypeItem Matter", "k": "Guide", "x": "In a plain ListView.builder, RenderSliverList has no idea how tall item 5,000 is without building and laying out items 0..4,999 or estimating from the few items currently mounted. Dragging a scrollbar thumb straight from top to bottom forces rough extent estimates — and scrollOffsetCorrection retries when scrolling back up. Whenever your items share uniform or predictable heights, tell Flutter up front: itemExtent: 56.0 switches from RenderSliverList to RenderSliverFixedExtentList. Item i is known to live at [i * 56.0, (i + 1) * 56.0] in $O(1)$ arithmetic — jumping to item 50,000 builds only the ~15 rows around index 50,000. prototypeItem: const MyRowPlaceholder() lays out one invisible prototype widget once to measure its height cleanly across dynamic font sizes, then uses that exact height with O(1) fixed-extent math. itemExtentBuilder: (index, dimensions) => ... gives $O(1)$ lookup when item heights vary by index (for example, section headers of 40px and rows of 72px) without building off-screen widgets."}, {"u": "guides/lazy-children.html", "a": "keeping-off-screen-state-alive", "p": "Lazy Children", "h": "Keeping Off-Screen State Alive", "k": "Guide", "x": "By default, SliverChildBuilderDelegate wraps every item in AutomaticKeepAlive (addAutomaticKeepAlives: true). An item's State can opt out of destruction when scrolled past cacheExtent by mixing in AutomaticKeepAliveClientMixin: When collectGarbage tries to evict a child whose SliverMultiBoxAdaptorParentData.keepAlive is true, it detaches the child from the active paint/layout linked list and parks it in _keepAliveBucket instead of unmounting its Element. Its State (and TextEditingController cursor!) stays intact in memory at zero per-frame layout/paint cost, and re-attaches instantly if scrolled back into view."}, {"u": "guides/lazy-children.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Lazy Children", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Returning wantKeepAlive => true unconditionally on hundreds of heavy items. Every kept-alive item retains its full Element, State, RenderBox subtree, and decoded image references in _keepAliveBucket. Tie wantKeepAlive to an actual condition (such as unsaved input) and call updateKeepAlive() once it clears. Reordering or deleting items in ListView.builder without Keys (or findChildIndexCallback). SliverMultiBoxAdaptorElement keys active children by integer index unless told otherwise. If you delete index 0 from a stateful list without keys, old index 0's State stays mounted and receives new index 0's widget data. Pass a stable ValueKey on each item (and findChildIndexCallback if using kept-alive items so Flutter can relocate parked elements to their new index). That's lazy child virtualization end to end: RenderSliverMultiBoxAdaptor builds children mid-layout across the visible + cacheExtent window, collectGarbage unmounts or buckets off-cache items, and itemExtent / prototypeItem turn layout math into $O(1)$ index arithmetic. Next, we'll look at headers that refuse to scroll off-screen: PinnedHeaderSliver, SliverResizingHeader, SliverFloatingHeader, and SliverPersistentHeader."}, {"u": "guides/pinned-headers.html", "a": "", "p": "Pinned and Floating Headers", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "Choosing between PinnedHeaderSliver, SliverResizingHeader, SliverFloatingHeader, SliverPersistentHeader, and SliverAppBar."}, {"u": "guides/pinned-headers.html", "a": "", "p": "Pinned and Floating Headers", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through Flutter's header slivers. By the end, you'll know which of the four focused header slivers (PinnedHeaderSliver, SliverResizingHeader, SliverFloatingHeader, and SliverPersistentHeader) fits each UI pattern, and how all of them work under the hood using layoutExtent, paintExtent, and overlap. We'll skip NestedScrollView overlap injection for now — this is about single-viewport headers, and only single-viewport headers."}, {"u": "guides/pinned-headers.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Pinned and Floating Headers", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "One Trick Behind Every Sticky Header — How shrinking layoutExtent while keeping paintExtent lets content slide underneath. The Three Simple Header Widgets — PinnedHeaderSliver, SliverResizingHeader, and SliverFloatingHeader (and why Flutter added them). SliverPersistentHeader and SliverAppBar — When you need shrinkOffset-driven custom builds or full Material app bar chrome. Stacking Multiple Headers with overlap — What happens when two pinned slivers meet at the top edge. Common Pitfalls — Forgetting shouldRebuild on a delegate, hardcoding heights that clip large fonts, and obstructing ensureVisible."}, {"u": "guides/pinned-headers.html", "a": "one-trick-behind-every-sticky-header", "p": "Pinned and Floating Headers", "h": "One Trick Behind Every Sticky Header", "k": "Guide", "x": "Every pinned or floating header in Flutter relies on one distinction in SliverGeometry: paintExtent is how tall the header draws on screen. layoutExtent is how far down the header pushes the next sliver. While a 56px header is still scrolling toward the top edge (constraints.scrollOffset == 0), both paintExtent and layoutExtent are 56.0. Once earlier slivers scroll away and constraints.scrollOffset climbs from 0 to 56, paintExtent stays 56.0 while layoutExtent shrinks from 56.0 down to 0.0. The sliver right after the header now lays out starting at y = 0 — sliding underneath the header — while the header continues painting on top of it. You can read and interact with every line of that calculation in the Sticky Header Sliver example."}, {"u": "guides/pinned-headers.html", "a": "the-three-simple-header-widgets", "p": "Pinned and Floating Headers", "h": "The Three Simple Header Widgets", "k": "Guide", "x": "For years, pinning any widget inside a CustomScrollView forced you to write a verbose SliverPersistentHeaderDelegate subclass with hardcoded minExtent and maxExtent numbers. Modern Flutter ships three composable widgets that measure their child automatically without a delegate class: PinnedHeaderSliver — Takes any single child widget, measures its natural box height during layout, scrolls normally until reaching the viewport's leading edge, and pins there permanently. SliverResizingHeader — Smoothly shrinks a child between the natural height of a maxExtentPrototype widget and a minExtentPrototype widget as the user scrolls, then stays pinned at minExtentPrototype's height. SliverFloatingHeader — Scrolls off-screen as the user scrolls down, and immediately slides back into view at the top of the viewport as soon as the user reverses direction (userScrollDirection == ScrollDirection.forward), snapping cleanly to fully shown or fully hidden when scrolling stops. Reach for PinnedHeaderSliver, SliverResizingHeader, or SliverFloatingHeader first. Because they measure child widgets directly during layout instead of asking you for hardcoded double extents, they adapt automatically when users enable large accessibility text scales."}, {"u": "guides/pinned-headers.html", "a": "sliverpersistentheader-and-sliverappbar", "p": "Pinned and Floating Headers", "h": "SliverPersistentHeader and SliverAppBar", "k": "Guide", "x": "You still want SliverPersistentHeader (or Material's SliverAppBar, which wraps SliverPersistentHeader internally) in two situations: Your header needs the exact pixel shrinkOffset and overlapsContent boolean during build to cross-fade titles, scale avatars, or toggle elevation shadows. You want SliverAppBar's built-in combinations of pinned, floating, snap, stretch (overscroll zoom), and FlexibleSpaceBar."}, {"u": "guides/pinned-headers.html", "a": "stacking-multiple-headers-with-overlap", "p": "Pinned and Floating Headers", "h": "Stacking Multiple Headers with overlap", "k": "Guide", "x": "What happens if you place two PinnedHeaderSlivers inside the same CustomScrollView? When the first 56px header is pinned, its paintExtent (56.0) exceeds its layoutExtent (0.0) by 56.0. RenderViewport passes that 56.0 difference into the next sliver as constraints.overlap. PinnedHeaderSliver sets paintOrigin: constraints.overlap. As a result, the second PinnedHeaderSliver pins at y = 56.0 right below the first header rather than painting directly over it! (If instead you want section headers that push the previous section's header off-screen, packages like sliver_tools or a custom multi-sliver coordinate their paintOrigins accordingly.)"}, {"u": "guides/pinned-headers.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Pinned and Floating Headers", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Returning false from SliverPersistentHeaderDelegate.shouldRebuild when fields changed. If shouldRebuild always returns false, rebuilding the parent widget with a new title or theme color leaves the header showing stale UI until shrinkOffset happens to change. Compare every field your build, minExtent, or maxExtent reads. Hardcoding minExtent / maxExtent smaller than the child's text height. In SliverPersistentHeaderDelegate, your build output is forced into tight height constraints between minExtent and maxExtent. Under 200% system text scaling, a hardcoded 48.0 header with two lines of text will overflow vertically; prefer PinnedHeaderSliver or include MediaQuery.textScalerOf(context) in your extent math. Using snap: true without floating: true on SliverAppBar. SliverAppBar asserts that snap requires floating: true (and a vsync ticker), because snapping animates the floating header open or closed when the user lifts their finger mid-reveal. That's pinned, resizing, and floating headers end to end: one layoutExtent vs paintExtent split, wrapped in three zero-boilerplate widgets for everyday layouts and SliverPersistentHeader / SliverAppBar when you need shrinkOffset-driven transitions. Next, we'll step back and see how ListView, GridView, CustomScrollView, and SingleChildScrollView assemble everything we've covered so far."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-views.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Views", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "How ListView, GridView, CustomScrollView, PageView, and SingleChildScrollView map onto the exact same building blocks."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-views.html", "a": "", "p": "Scroll Views", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through Flutter's high-level scroll view widgets (CustomScrollView, ListView, GridView, PageView, and SingleChildScrollView). By the end, you'll see that ListView and GridView are thin 20-line presets over CustomScrollView, know how padding and SliverSafeArea actually inject slivers, and choose the right scroll view on the first try. We'll skip nested coordination and 2D grids until the next group — this is about standard scroll views, and only standard scroll views."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-views.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Scroll Views", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "ScrollView Is Just a Factory for Scrollable + Viewport — What ScrollView.build actually constructs. ListView and GridView Are Single-Sliver CustomScrollViews — What buildSlivers returns for each constructor. How padding Works Inside BoxScrollView — Why ListView(padding: ...) wraps its sliver in SliverPadding instead of Padding. SingleChildScrollView vs ListView — Choosing between box-based repaint scrolling and sliver virtualization. Choosing the Right Widget — A quick decision table for real screens. Common Pitfalls — Wrapping slivers in Padding, using ListView(children: ...) for large lists, and PageView off-screen building."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-views.html", "a": "scrollview-is-just-a-factory-for-scrollable--viewport", "p": "Scroll Views", "h": "ScrollView Is Just a Factory for Scrollable + Viewport", "k": "Guide", "x": "Open packages/flutter/lib/src/widgets/scroll_view.dart and you'll find a pleasant surprise: ScrollView is a StatelessWidget. It holds no state of its own. Its build method does three small things: Resolves PrimaryScrollController if controller == null and the view is primary. Calls buildSlivers(context) to get the List<Widget> of slivers. Returns a Scrollable whose viewportBuilder builds either a Viewport (normal) or a ShrinkWrappingViewport (shrinkWrap: true). CustomScrollView is the most direct subclass of ScrollView in the entire framework — its buildSlivers method literally returns slivers!"}, {"u": "guides/scroll-views.html", "a": "listview-and-gridview-are-single-sliver-customscrollviews", "p": "Scroll Views", "h": "ListView and GridView Are Single-Sliver CustomScrollViews", "k": "Guide", "x": "ListView and GridView both extend BoxScrollView (which extends ScrollView). Neither has a special viewport or special gesture handling. Each constructor simply picks one sliver inside buildChildLayout: ListView(...) / ListView.builder(...) SliverList (when no extent is given) ListView(..., itemExtent: h) SliverFixedExtentList ListView(..., prototypeItem: w) SliverPrototypeExtentList ListView(..., itemExtentBuilder: fn) SliverVariedExtentList ListView.separated(...) SliverList with 2 * n - 1 children interleaving items and separators GridView.count / .extent / .builder SliverGrid Graduating from ListView to CustomScrollView changes zero scrolling behavior — it just lets buildSlivers return two or more slivers (say, a PinnedHeaderSliver, a SliverPadding around a SliverGrid, and a SliverList) inside one shared Viewport and one shared ScrollPosition."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-views.html", "a": "how-padding-works-inside-boxscrollview", "p": "Scroll Views", "h": "How padding Works Inside BoxScrollView", "k": "Guide", "x": "Notice SliverPadding in the snippet above. If you wrapped a ListView in a normal Padding(padding: EdgeInsets.all(16)), the viewport window itself would shrink by 16px on every side, clipping scrolling items 16px away from the screen edges instead of letting them scroll cleanly behind the top and bottom status/navigation bars! BoxScrollView (ListView and GridView) handles padding properly: If you pass padding:, BoxScrollView.buildSlivers wraps the inner sliver in SliverPadding(padding: padding!, sliver: sliver). If you leave padding: null, BoxScrollView checks MediaQuery.maybePaddingOf(context) along the scroll axis (for example, the iOS notch and home indicator) and automatically inserts a SliverPadding with those safe-area insets! When you switch from ListView to CustomScrollView, BoxScrollView's automatic MediaQuery padding is no longer inserted for you. Wrap slivers that need safe-area spacing in SliverSafeArea or SliverPadding explicitly."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-views.html", "a": "singlechildscrollview-vs-listview", "p": "Scroll Views", "h": "SingleChildScrollView vs ListView", "k": "Guide", "x": "SingleChildScrollView does not extend ScrollView and does not use slivers. It pairs Scrollable with _SingleChildViewport (a single-child RenderBox viewport just like Minimal Viewport). Use SingleChildScrollView when: You have a bounded form, settings card, or dialog body with a handful of heterogeneous widgets that might overflow on a small phone or when the soft keyboard opens. You need IntrinsicWidth or CrossAxisAlignment.stretch across the whole column without sliver ceremony. Use ListView.builder / CustomScrollView whenever child count scales with data or items contain images and heavy subtrees."}, {"u": "guides/scroll-views.html", "a": "choosing-the-right-widget", "p": "Scroll Views", "h": "Choosing the Right Widget", "k": "Guide", "x": "One short form that scrolls when the keyboard opens → SingleChildScrollView One uniform list of items → ListView.builder (pass itemExtent or prototypeItem if heights match) Header + grid + list scrolling together → CustomScrollView Full-viewport or fractional-viewport swipeable pages → PageView.builder (uses Viewport + SliverFillViewport + PageScrollPhysics + PageController)"}, {"u": "guides/scroll-views.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Scroll Views", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Passing a normal RenderBox widget directly into CustomScrollView.slivers. Every direct child of CustomScrollView.slivers must produce a RenderSliver. Passing a Container or Padding throws \"A RenderViewport expected a child of type RenderSliver but received a child of type RenderPadding\". Use SliverToBoxAdapter for single boxes and SliverPadding for insets. Using the default ListView(children: [...]) constructor with a .map(...).toList() over hundreds of items. Even though ListView(children: ...) still mounts elements lazily via SliverChildListDelegate, Dart evaluates your .map(...).toList() eagerly on every parent rebuild, allocating all Widget instances up front. Use ListView.builder so only visible indices allocate widgets. That wraps up the Slivers group! You now know the whole core scrolling stack from Scrollable down to SliverList and CustomScrollView. The final three guides in Going Further tackle specialized topics you can read whenever you hit them: programmatic reveal (ensureVisible), NestedScrollView, and 2D scrolling."}, {"u": "guides/revealing-content.html", "a": "", "p": "Revealing Content", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "How Scrollable.ensureVisible, getOffsetToReveal, and showOnScreen scroll a target into view — and why unmounted lazy items can't be found by BuildContext."}, {"u": "guides/revealing-content.html", "a": "", "p": "Revealing Content", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through Flutter's reveal pipeline: Scrollable.ensureVisible, RenderAbstractViewport.getOffsetToReveal, and RenderObject.showOnScreen. By the end, you'll understand how Flutter computes the exact scroll offset needed to bring any mounted widget into view, how alignment and alignmentPolicy differ, and why ensureVisible cannot jump to an unbuilt item in a lazy ListView.builder. We'll skip custom physics simulations — this is about finding and scrolling to a target widget, and only that."}, {"u": "guides/revealing-content.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Revealing Content", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "Two Entry Points: ensureVisible and showOnScreen — Widget-level reveal vs render-tree auto-scroll (used by focused TextFields). How getOffsetToReveal Computes the Target Offset — Walking from target RenderObject up to RenderAbstractViewport. alignment vs alignmentPolicy — Pinning to a specific fraction of the viewport vs scrolling only the minimum distance required. Why Lazy Lists Can't Reveal Unmounted Items — What happens when item 400 has no BuildContext yet, and the three clean alternatives. Common Pitfalls — Hidden behind a custom overlay, soft keyboard race conditions, and calling ensureVisible on an unmounted GlobalKey."}, {"u": "guides/revealing-content.html", "a": "two-entry-points-ensurevisible-and-showonscreen", "p": "Revealing Content", "h": "Two Entry Points: ensureVisible and showOnScreen", "k": "Guide", "x": "Flutter has two closely related ways to scroll a descendant into view: Scrollable.ensureVisible(context, ...) is the explicit widget API you call from app code. Starting at context, it walks every enclosing Scrollable (both inner horizontal carousels and outer vertical lists!) and calls position.ensureVisible(...) on each one, returning a Future<void> when all animations complete. RenderObject.showOnScreen(...) is the low-level render tree walk triggered automatically when a TextField gains focus or an accessibility screen reader moves focus to an off-screen node. Each parent render object translates the target Rect into its own coordinate space until RenderViewportBase.showInViewport adjusts offset."}, {"u": "guides/revealing-content.html", "a": "how-getoffsettoreveal-computes-the-target-offset", "p": "Revealing Content", "h": "How getOffsetToReveal Computes the Target Offset", "k": "Guide", "x": "When ScrollPosition.ensureVisible runs on a target RenderObject, how does it know which pixels value puts that object on screen? It looks up RenderAbstractViewport.of(object) and calls viewport.getOffsetToReveal(object, alignment): RenderViewport walks up from object to the direct child RenderSliver that contains it, accumulating the box's paint offset inside that sliver. It adds the sliver's layout position relative to the scroll origin (precedingScrollExtent in forward slivers). If earlier pinned headers reported geometry.maxScrollObstructionExtent (for example, a 56px PinnedHeaderSliver), getOffsetToReveal subtracts that obstructed band from the usable viewport height so alignment: 0.0 lands cleanly below the pinned header instead of tucked behind it!"}, {"u": "guides/revealing-content.html", "a": "alignment-vs-alignmentpolicy", "p": "Revealing Content", "h": "alignment vs alignmentPolicy", "k": "Guide", "x": "By default, ScrollPositionAlignmentPolicy.explicit always moves the scroll view so the target sits at alignment (0.0 = flush with the top/left edge, 0.5 = dead center, 1.0 = flush with the bottom/right edge) — even if the widget was already completely visible on screen. For keyboard arrow navigation or form validation, jumping an already-visible field to the top edge feels jarring. Pass one of the two minimal-movement policies instead: ScrollPositionAlignmentPolicy.keepVisibleAtEnd — If the target is below the bottom edge, scrolls just enough to reveal it at the trailing edge (1.0); if already fully visible, does nothing. ScrollPositionAlignmentPolicy.keepVisibleAtStart — If the target is above the top edge, scrolls just enough to reveal it at the leading edge (0.0); if already fully visible, does nothing."}, {"u": "guides/revealing-content.html", "a": "why-lazy-lists-cant-reveal-unmounted-items", "p": "Revealing Content", "h": "Why Lazy Lists Can't Reveal Unmounted Items", "k": "Guide", "x": "Here is the fundamental limitation of Scrollable.ensureVisible: it requires a live BuildContext (and mounted RenderObject) for the target. In a ListView.builder with 1,000 items where rows 0..12 are on screen, row 500 has not been built yet — itemKeys[500].currentContext is null! You have three clean ways to jump to index 500 depending on your layout: Uniform or predictable heights: Set itemExtent, prototypeItem, or itemExtentBuilder on your ListView and compute the exact offset directly (controller.animateTo(500 * itemExtent, ...)). Bounded item count (< 100 items): Use SingleChildScrollView + Column if every item is lightweight and already mounted anyway. Arbitrary variable-height thousands-item lists (chat jump-to-message): Anchor CustomScrollView(center: targetSliverKey) around the jumped-to slice, or use an indexed list package (scrollable_positioned_list / super_sliver_list) that maintains an index-to-sliver-offset map."}, {"u": "guides/revealing-content.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Revealing Content", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Calling ensureVisible right when a TextField gains focus before the keyboard finishes animating. While the soft keyboard slides up over ~250ms, MediaQuery.viewInsets.bottom grows frame by frame. EditableText already schedules showOnScreen automatically via WidgetsBindingObserver.didChangeMetrics; if you call a manual ensureVisible in onTap, it runs before the viewport shrinks for the keyboard and stops too high. Set Scaffold.resizeToAvoidBottomInset: true and TextField.scrollPadding instead. Floating overlays (Stack headers) hiding revealed items. getOffsetToReveal only knows about obstructions inside the Viewport (PinnedHeaderSliver, SliverAppBar). If you float a translucent bar outside the CustomScrollView inside a Stack, ensureVisible(alignment: 0.0) will place your item right underneath that Stack overlay. Either move the bar into a sliver (PinnedHeaderSliver) or pad the scroll view with SliverPadding(padding: EdgeInsets.only(top: barHeight)). That's content reveal end to end: Scrollable.ensureVisible walks ancestor scrollables, getOffsetToReveal translates target render bounds into a pixels offset while respecting sliver obstructions, and itemExtent arithmetic handles unmounted lazy indices. Next, we'll look at NestedScrollView — how one drag smoothly collapses an outer header and then scrolls an inner TabBarView list."}, {"u": "guides/nested-scrolling.html", "a": "", "p": "Nested Scrolling", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "When you actually need NestedScrollView, how its coordinator splits one gesture across two positions, and why SliverOverlapAbsorber exists."}, {"u": "guides/nested-scrolling.html", "a": "", "p": "Nested Scrolling", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through NestedScrollView and nested scroll coordination. By the end, you'll know when a single CustomScrollView already solves your layout without nesting, how _NestedScrollCoordinator splits drags and flings between an outer header and inner tab lists, and why pinned headers inside NestedScrollView need SliverOverlapAbsorber + SliverOverlapInjector. We'll skip custom 2D viewports — this is about coupled outer/inner vertical scrolling, and only that."}, {"u": "guides/nested-scrolling.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Nested Scrolling", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "Do You Actually Need NestedScrollView? — Why 80% of \"nested scroll\" screens are really just one CustomScrollView with multiple slivers. The One Case That Truly Needs It — Collapsing outer header + horizontally swipeable TabBarView where each tab scrolls independently. How _NestedScrollCoordinator Splits a Drag — One gesture driving _outerPosition first and _innerPositions second. SliverOverlapAbsorber and SliverOverlapInjector — Forwarding outer pinned-header overlap across the viewport boundary. Common Pitfalls — Passing a custom controller to the inner ListView, forgetting PageStorageKey on tabs, and putting orthogonal carousels inside NestedScrollView."}, {"u": "guides/nested-scrolling.html", "a": "do-you-actually-need-nestedscrollview", "p": "Nested Scrolling", "h": "Do You Actually Need NestedScrollView?", "k": "Guide", "x": "Before reaching for NestedScrollView, check what you are putting inside what: Horizontal PageView or card strip inside a vertical ListView? You do not need NestedScrollView. Flutter's gesture arena automatically disambiguates perpendicular axes (HorizontalDragGestureRecognizer vs VerticalDragGestureRecognizer). A banner, a grid, and two lists scrolling together vertically? You do not need NestedScrollView. Put SliverToBoxAdapter, SliverGrid, and two SliverLists inside a single CustomScrollView."}, {"u": "guides/nested-scrolling.html", "a": "the-one-case-that-truly-needs-it", "p": "Nested Scrolling", "h": "The One Case That Truly Needs It", "k": "Guide", "x": "Suppose a profile screen has a 240px collapsing header + pinned TabBar at the top, and a swipeable TabBarView (\"Posts\", \"Media\", \"Likes\") filling the body below. A single CustomScrollView cannot contain a TabBarView whose children are slivers — TabBarView is a RenderBox widget (PageView) that clips and slides whole pages horizontally, and each tab wants to remember its own vertical scroll position! That requires two viewports: an outer CustomScrollView for the collapsible header, and an inner ScrollView inside each tab. NestedScrollView's coordinator routes upward drags to collapse the outer header first, then scrolls the active inner tab list."}, {"u": "guides/nested-scrolling.html", "a": "how-nestedscrollcoordinator-splits-a-drag", "p": "Nested Scrolling", "h": "How _NestedScrollCoordinator Splits a Drag", "k": "Guide", "x": "If you simply placed a ListView inside the body of a normal ScrollView, the inner ListView would win the vertical drag arena and scroll while the outer header sat frozen at the top. NestedScrollView solves this with a private _NestedScrollCoordinator that intercepts Scrollable input and owns custom _NestedScrollPosition instances for both the outer header and every mounted inner tab: Scrolling down (delta < 0, finger moving up): The coordinator feeds the delta to _outerPosition first until _outerPosition reaches its maxScrollExtent (the header is fully collapsed), then feeds every remaining pixel of that same drag frame to the active inner position! Scrolling up (delta > 0, finger moving down): Under floatHeaderSlivers: false (default), the coordinator scrolls the inner list all the way back to 0.0 first, and only then expands the outer header. With floatHeaderSlivers: true, reversing direction immediately pulls the floating outer header back into view first. Liftoff flings: One ballistic simulation is split across both positions so flicking upward collapses the header and seamlessly continues coasting down the inner ListView. For the inner ListView to register with _NestedScrollCoordinator, the inner ListView must inherit NestedScrollView's PrimaryScrollController — do not pass your own ScrollController to the inner ListView!"}, {"u": "guides/nested-scrolling.html", "a": "sliveroverlapabsorber-and-sliveroverlapinjector", "p": "Nested Scrolling", "h": "SliverOverlapAbsorber and SliverOverlapInjector", "k": "Guide", "x": "When an outer SliverAppBar(pinned: true) stays pinned at the top of NestedScrollView, its SliverGeometry reports overlap inside the outer viewport. The inner CustomScrollView lives inside a separate viewport in body, so it has no idea the top 104px of its space is covered by the pinned outer app bar and TabBar! Without help, row 0 of each tab renders hidden behind the pinned bar. SliverOverlapAbsorber and SliverOverlapInjector bridge that gap across the two viewports: SliverOverlapAbsorber swallows the pinned header's overlap in the outer viewport and writes its pixel height into a shared SliverOverlapAbsorberHandle; SliverOverlapInjector sits first inside each inner CustomScrollView and expands by that exact pixel height so row 0 starts right below the TabBar."}, {"u": "guides/nested-scrolling.html", "a": "common-pitfalls", "p": "Nested Scrolling", "h": "Common Pitfalls", "k": "Guide", "x": "Passing a ScrollController to an inner ListView inside NestedScrollView.body. Passing ListView(controller: myController) disconnects that list from the PrimaryScrollController supplied by NestedScrollView, breaking coordinator handoff completely. Control the outer view via NestedScrollView(controller: ...) or read NestedScrollViewState.innerController via a GlobalKey<NestedScrollViewState>. Calling NestedScrollView.sliverOverlapAbsorberHandleFor(context) with the wrong context. Inside body, you must look up the handle using a Builder under NestedScrollView (not the outer build method's context above NestedScrollView), or Flutter won't find the ancestor _InheritedNestedScrollView. That's NestedScrollView end to end: reach for a single CustomScrollView whenever possible, and when a TabBarView truly forces two vertical viewports, let _NestedScrollCoordinator drive the inner views via PrimaryScrollController while SliverOverlapAbsorber + SliverOverlapInjector keep pinned headers aligned. Our final guide covers scrolling along both axes simultaneously: TwoDimensionalScrollView."}, {"u": "guides/two-dimensional-scrolling.html", "a": "", "p": "Two-Dimensional Scrolling", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "How TwoDimensionalScrollView, TwoDimensionalScrollable, and RenderTwoDimensionalViewport virtualize both axes without slivers."}, {"u": "guides/two-dimensional-scrolling.html", "a": "", "p": "Two-Dimensional Scrolling", "h": "", "k": "Guide", "x": "This guide walks experienced Flutter developers through Flutter's 2D scrolling foundation: TwoDimensionalScrollView, TwoDimensionalScrollable, TwoDimensionalViewport, and ChildVicinity. By the end, you'll understand why slivers are strictly one-dimensional, how Flutter wires two perpendicular ScrollPositions into a single viewport, and how layoutChildSequence virtualizes a 10,000 × 10,000 grid in $O(\\text{visible cells})$. We'll skip two_dimensional_scrollables package styling options — this is about the core framework architecture, and only that architecture."}, {"u": "guides/two-dimensional-scrolling.html", "a": "what-this-guide-covers", "p": "Two-Dimensional Scrolling", "h": "What This Guide Covers", "k": "Guide", "x": "Why Slivers Can't Virtualize Two Axes at Once — What goes wrong when you nest horizontal ListViews inside a vertical ListView. Two Scrollables, One TwoDimensionalViewport — How TwoDimensionalScrollable pairs a vertical ScrollPosition and a horizontal ScrollPosition. DiagonalDragBehavior — Controlling whether diagonal finger drags pan freely, lock to the first axis, or lock per gesture. Addressing Cells with ChildVicinity — Replacing 1D int index with (xIndex, yIndex) inside TwoDimensionalChildDelegate. How RenderTwoDimensionalViewport Works — Building, positioning, and garbage-collecting RenderBox cells in layoutChildSequence. When to Use two_dimensional_scrollables Instead of Subclassing — TableView and TreeView built on top of these primitives."}, {"u": "guides/two-dimensional-scrolling.html", "a": "why-slivers-cant-virtualize-two-axes-at-once", "p": "Two-Dimensional Scrolling", "h": "Why Slivers Can't Virtualize Two Axes at Once", "k": "Guide", "x": "Look back at SliverConstraints: it has one scrollOffset, one remainingPaintExtent, and one fixed crossAxisExtent. Slivers are 1D by design. If you fake a spreadsheet by putting a horizontal SingleChildScrollView around a vertical ListView, every row builds all of its columns eagerly; if you put a horizontal ListView inside each vertical row, each row scrolls horizontally on its own instead of moving the whole table together! True 2D virtualization needs one viewport that knows both horizontalOffset.pixels and verticalOffset.pixels at the same time."}, {"u": "guides/two-dimensional-scrolling.html", "a": "two-scrollables-one-twodimensionalviewport", "p": "Two-Dimensional Scrolling", "h": "Two Scrollables, One TwoDimensionalViewport", "k": "Guide", "x": "Open two_dimensional_scrollable.dart and you'll find a remarkably clean reuse of the 1D machinery you already learned: TwoDimensionalScrollable simply nests two ordinary Scrollable widgets — an outer vertical Scrollable and an inner horizontal Scrollable — and hands both of their ViewportOffsets (verticalOffset and horizontalOffset) into a single TwoDimensionalViewport: Because both axes are driven by real Scrollables, you reuse standard ScrollControllers, BouncingScrollPhysics / ClampingScrollPhysics, Scrollbars, and ScrollNotifications on each axis without reinventing any gesture or physics code!"}, {"u": "guides/two-dimensional-scrolling.html", "a": "diagonaldragbehavior", "p": "Two-Dimensional Scrolling", "h": "DiagonalDragBehavior", "k": "Guide", "x": "When two perpendicular Scrollables wrap the same surface, what should happen when a user drags their finger at a 35° diagonal? TwoDimensionalScrollView.diagonalDragBehavior offers four built-in modes: DiagonalDragBehavior.none (default) — Only one axis wins any single drag gesture; dragging mostly vertically scrolls only vertically until you lift your finger. DiagonalDragBehavior.weightedEvent — Locks to horizontal or vertical per pointer-move sample based on the dominant delta. DiagonalDragBehavior.weightedContinuous — Locks to the initial dominant axis unless the user sharply changes direction mid-drag. DiagonalDragBehavior.free — Both VerticalDragGestureRecognizer and HorizontalDragGestureRecognizer accept the gesture simultaneously, panning smoothly across (x, y) like a map or infinite canvas."}, {"u": "guides/two-dimensional-scrolling.html", "a": "addressing-cells-with-childvicinity", "p": "Two-Dimensional Scrolling", "h": "Addressing Cells with ChildVicinity", "k": "Guide", "x": "Because there are no slivers inside a TwoDimensionalViewport, cells are plain RenderBox widgets addressed by a 2D coordinate struct called ChildVicinity: TwoDimensionalChildBuilderDelegate (the 2D twin of SliverChildBuilderDelegate) builds widgets on demand for (BuildContext context, ChildVicinity vicinity), bounded optionally by maxXIndex and maxYIndex."}, {"u": "guides/two-dimensional-scrolling.html", "a": "how-rendertwodimensionalviewport-works", "p": "Two-Dimensional Scrolling", "h": "How RenderTwoDimensionalViewport Works", "k": "Guide", "x": "In the Flutter SDK itself, TwoDimensionalScrollView, TwoDimensionalViewport, and RenderTwoDimensionalViewport are abstract base classes. To define a concrete 2D layout (fixed grid, variable spreadsheet with pinned row/column headers, or staggered canvas), a subclass implements one method on RenderTwoDimensionalViewport: layoutChildSequence(). Inside layoutChildSequence(), your viewport does four things: Reads horizontalOffset.pixels, verticalOffset.pixels, viewportDimension (Size), and cacheExtent to decide which (xIndex, yIndex) cells fall inside the visible + cache rectangle. Calls buildOrObtainChildFor(ChildVicinity(xIndex: x, yIndex: y)) for only those visible cells (which builds new widgets mid-layout via invokeLayoutCallback or reuses mounted ones). Lays out each obtained RenderBox child and sets parentDataOf(child).layoutOffset = Offset(cellX - horizontalOffset.pixels, cellY - verticalOffset.pixels). Reports total 2D bounds via verticalOffset.applyContentDimensions(...) and horizontalOffset.applyContentDimensions(...). Any previously mounted cell that was not visited by buildOrObtainChildFor during layoutChildSequence() is automatically garbage-collected (or parked in the keep-alive bucket) by RenderTwoDimensionalViewport.performLayout right after layoutChildSequence() returns!"}, {"u": "guides/two-dimensional-scrolling.html", "a": "when-to-use-twodimensionalscrollables-instead-of-subclassing", "p": "Two-Dimensional Scrolling", "h": "When to Use two_dimensional_scrollables Instead of Subclassing", "k": "Guide", "x": "You only need to subclass TwoDimensionalScrollView and RenderTwoDimensionalViewport yourself when inventing a novel 2D spatial layout (such as an infinite node graph, hex map, or custom timeline gantt chart). For data tables, spreadsheets with pinned rows and columns, or lazy 2D tree views, the Flutter team's official package:two_dimensional_scrollables provides ready-to-use TableView and TreeView widgets built directly on RenderTwoDimensionalViewport."}, {"u": "guides/two-dimensional-scrolling.html", "a": "mental-model-summary", "p": "Two-Dimensional Scrolling", "h": "Mental Model Summary", "k": "Guide", "x": "Slivers are 1D; TwoDimensionalViewport skips slivers completely and manages RenderBox children directly by ChildVicinity(xIndex, yIndex). Two standard Scrollables drive verticalOffset and horizontalOffset independently, coordinated by DiagonalDragBehavior. layoutChildSequence() visits only cells inside the 2D visible + cache window; unvisited cells are garbage-collected automatically at the end of every layout pass. That completes every guide on this site. To see custom physics, a custom viewport, and a custom sliver written out line by line with live interactive demos, head to the Examples next."}, {"u": "api/index.html", "a": "", "p": "API Reference", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Reference documentation for the key methods and types you use when customizing scrolling."}, {"u": "api/index.html", "a": "", "p": "API Reference", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "It's easy to forget the exact sign convention of a physics method, or which SliverGeometry fields must agree with each other. These API docs give targeted, recipe-style instructions for each method and data contract you touch when building custom scrolling behavior. To build a clear mental model of the what, why, and how of scrolling, see the Guides section. To see these methods working together in real implementations, see the Examples section."}, {"u": "api/apply-to.html", "a": "", "p": "applyTo()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Implement applyTo() in a custom ScrollPhysics subclass to preserve your physics in the chain."}, {"u": "api/apply-to.html", "a": "", "p": "applyTo()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "The applyTo(ScrollPhysics? ancestor) method clones your ScrollPhysics subclass with ancestor chained onto the end of its parent list. Scrollable calls applyTo whenever it merges widget-level physics with ambient ScrollBehavior physics."}, {"u": "api/apply-to.html", "a": "default-implementation", "p": "applyTo()", "h": "Default Implementation", "k": "API", "x": "The base ScrollPhysics.applyTo returns a plain ScrollPhysics instance, which drops any subclass overrides if you forget to override it:"}, {"u": "api/apply-to.html", "a": "stateless-subclass---no-extra-fields", "p": "applyTo()", "h": "Stateless Subclass - No Extra Fields", "k": "API", "x": "Example where your ScrollPhysics subclass has no constructor parameters beyond super.parent. Always compute parent: via buildParent(ancestor) rather than passing ancestor directly so existing parents on this are preserved."}, {"u": "api/apply-to.html", "a": "parameterized-subclass---copy-fields-and-implement-shouldupdate", "p": "applyTo()", "h": "Parameterized Subclass - Copy Fields and Implement shouldUpdate", "k": "API", "x": "Example where your ScrollPhysics subclass carries configuration fields. Copy every field in applyTo and compare them in shouldUpdate so ScrollableState rebuilds the physics chain when a parameter changes."}, {"u": "api/apply-to.html", "a": "composing-two-physics-classes-at-a-call-site", "p": "applyTo()", "h": "Composing Two Physics Classes at a Call Site", "k": "API", "x": "Example of chaining two built-in ScrollPhysics instances from widget code. Either pass parent: in a const constructor or call applyTo dynamically."}, {"u": "api/apply-physics-to-user-offset.html", "a": "", "p": "applyPhysicsToUserOffset()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Implement applyPhysicsToUserOffset() in a custom ScrollPhysics to scale or resist finger drags."}, {"u": "api/apply-physics-to-user-offset.html", "a": "", "p": "applyPhysicsToUserOffset()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "The applyPhysicsToUserOffset(ScrollMetrics position, double offset) method converts a raw pointer drag delta (offset, in logical pixels along the scroll axis) into the delta that ScrollPosition will subtract from pixels. A positive offset means dragging forward (down/right, decreasing pixels). A negative offset means dragging in reverse (up/left, increasing pixels)."}, {"u": "api/apply-physics-to-user-offset.html", "a": "default-implementation", "p": "applyPhysicsToUserOffset()", "h": "Default Implementation", "k": "API", "x": "Delegates to parent if present, or returns offset unchanged (1:1 finger tracking):"}, {"u": "api/apply-physics-to-user-offset.html", "a": "constant-damping---heavy-scroll-feel", "p": "applyPhysicsToUserOffset()", "h": "Constant Damping - Heavy Scroll Feel", "k": "API", "x": "Example that halves user drag speed everywhere while preserving any overscroll friction applied by parent."}, {"u": "api/apply-physics-to-user-offset.html", "a": "rubber-band-resistance-when-overscrolled", "p": "applyPhysicsToUserOffset()", "h": "Rubber-Band Resistance When Overscrolled", "k": "API", "x": "Example that tracks 1:1 inside [minScrollExtent, maxScrollExtent] and progressively stiffens the drag once the user pulls past either edge."}, {"u": "api/apply-physics-to-user-offset.html", "a": "one-way-ratchet---block-dragging-backward", "p": "applyPhysicsToUserOffset()", "h": "One-Way Ratchet - Block Dragging Backward", "k": "API", "x": "Example where the user may drag down the list (offset < 0, increasing pixels) freely, while dragging back toward the top (offset > 0) is ignored."}, {"u": "api/apply-boundary-conditions.html", "a": "", "p": "applyBoundaryConditions()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Implement applyBoundaryConditions() in a custom ScrollPhysics to clamp or allow overscroll at edges."}, {"u": "api/apply-boundary-conditions.html", "a": "", "p": "applyBoundaryConditions()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "The applyBoundaryConditions(ScrollMetrics position, double value) method inspects a proposed new pixels offset (value) and returns the overscroll — the portion of value - position.pixels that is refused. ScrollPosition.setPixels computes actualPixels = value - overscroll. Returning 0.0 accepts the entire move; any non-zero return must have the same sign as value - position.pixels and magnitude at most (value - position.pixels).abs()."}, {"u": "api/apply-boundary-conditions.html", "a": "default-implementation", "p": "applyBoundaryConditions()", "h": "Default Implementation", "k": "API", "x": "Delegates to parent if present, or returns 0.0 (no boundaries enforced):"}, {"u": "api/apply-boundary-conditions.html", "a": "allow-free-overscroll-ios-bounce-style", "p": "applyBoundaryConditions()", "h": "Allow Free Overscroll (iOS Bounce Style)", "k": "API", "x": "Example where pixels is allowed to move freely past both minScrollExtent and maxScrollExtent so a spring simulation can pull it back later."}, {"u": "api/apply-boundary-conditions.html", "a": "hard-clamp-at-top-bounce-at-bottom", "p": "applyBoundaryConditions()", "h": "Hard Clamp at Top, Bounce at Bottom", "k": "API", "x": "Example that refuses any movement below minScrollExtent (0.0) at the top edge while delegating bottom-edge behavior to BouncingScrollPhysics."}, {"u": "api/apply-boundary-conditions.html", "a": "cap-maximum-overscroll-distance", "p": "applyBoundaryConditions()", "h": "Cap Maximum Overscroll Distance", "k": "API", "x": "Example that allows bouncing past the edges, up to a hard 80.0-pixel ceiling in either direction."}, {"u": "api/create-ballistic-simulation.html", "a": "", "p": "createBallisticSimulation()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Implement createBallisticSimulation() in a custom ScrollPhysics to drive flings, springs, and snapping after liftoff."}, {"u": "api/create-ballistic-simulation.html", "a": "", "p": "createBallisticSimulation()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "The createBallisticSimulation(ScrollMetrics position, double velocity) method returns the Simulation that drives pixels over time once the user lifts their finger (or after content bounds change). Return null when the position is already at rest where it belongs (IdleScrollActivity). Return a Simulation (ScrollSpringSimulation, ClampingScrollSimulation, FrictionSimulation, etc.) to start a BallisticScrollActivity."}, {"u": "api/create-ballistic-simulation.html", "a": "default-implementation", "p": "createBallisticSimulation()", "h": "Default Implementation", "k": "API", "x": "Delegates to parent if present, or returns null:"}, {"u": "api/create-ballistic-simulation.html", "a": "disable-coasting---stop-dead-on-finger-up", "p": "createBallisticSimulation()", "h": "Disable Coasting - Stop Dead on Finger Up", "k": "API", "x": "Example where lifting the finger stops scrolling immediately inside bounds, while still springing back if overscrolled past an edge."}, {"u": "api/create-ballistic-simulation.html", "a": "snap-to-fixed-intervals-scrollspringsimulation", "p": "createBallisticSimulation()", "h": "Snap to Fixed Intervals (ScrollSpringSimulation)", "k": "API", "x": "Example that lands on the nearest multiple of snapExtent, advancing by one step when velocity exceeds tolerance.velocity."}, {"u": "api/create-ballistic-simulation.html", "a": "custom-friction-drag-frictionsimulation", "p": "createBallisticSimulation()", "h": "Custom Friction Drag (FrictionSimulation)", "k": "API", "x": "Example that replaces platform deceleration with a custom drag coefficient (0.05 coasts further; 0.3 stops quickly) inside bounds."}, {"u": "api/jump-to.html", "a": "", "p": "jumpTo()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Use jumpTo() on ScrollController or ScrollPosition to teleport immediately to an exact pixel offset."}, {"u": "api/jump-to.html", "a": "", "p": "jumpTo()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "The jumpTo(double value) method cancels any active drag or ballistic fling, moves pixels straight to value without animation, dispatches start/update/end scroll notifications if pixels changed, and runs goBallistic(0.0) so an out-of-range jump still settles cleanly."}, {"u": "api/jump-to.html", "a": "default-implementation", "p": "jumpTo()", "h": "Default Implementation", "k": "API", "x": "On ScrollController, jumpTo forwards the call to every attached ScrollPosition:"}, {"u": "api/jump-to.html", "a": "jump-back-to-the-top", "p": "jumpTo()", "h": "Jump Back to the Top", "k": "API", "x": "Example that teleports to the leading edge only when a scrollable is mounted and attached."}, {"u": "api/jump-to.html", "a": "jump-to-the-very-bottom-after-layout", "p": "jumpTo()", "h": "Jump to the Very Bottom After Layout", "k": "API", "x": "Example that jumps to maxScrollExtent on the first frame after new items are added, when maxScrollExtent has been updated by layout."}, {"u": "api/jump-to.html", "a": "jump-to-a-fixed-extent-item-index", "p": "jumpTo()", "h": "Jump to a Fixed-Extent Item Index", "k": "API", "x": "Example where every row in a ListView has itemExtent: 56.0, clamped so indices near the end do not jump past maxScrollExtent."}, {"u": "api/animate-to.html", "a": "", "p": "animateTo()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Use animateTo() on ScrollController or ScrollPosition to smoothly drive pixels along a curve over a fixed duration."}, {"u": "api/animate-to.html", "a": "", "p": "animateTo()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "The animateTo(double offset, {required Duration duration, required Curve curve}) method starts a DrivenScrollActivity from the current pixels to offset and returns a Future<void> that completes when the animation finishes (or is interrupted by another gesture or activity)."}, {"u": "api/animate-to.html", "a": "default-implementation", "p": "animateTo()", "h": "Default Implementation", "k": "API", "x": "On ScrollController, animateTo starts an animation on every attached ScrollPosition and waits for all of them with Future.wait:"}, {"u": "api/animate-to.html", "a": "smooth-scroll-to-top", "p": "animateTo()", "h": "Smooth Scroll to Top", "k": "API", "x": "Example that animates back to 0.0 with an ease-out curve when the user taps a floating button."}, {"u": "api/animate-to.html", "a": "cap-travel-time-on-very-long-lists", "p": "animateTo()", "h": "Cap Travel Time on Very Long Lists", "k": "API", "x": "Example that jumps near the destination first when pixels is thousands of pixels away, then animates only the final two screenfuls so the user never sees a blurry 60-item flash."}, {"u": "api/animate-to.html", "a": "respect-reduced-motion-accessibility-preferences", "p": "animateTo()", "h": "Respect Reduced-Motion Accessibility Preferences", "k": "API", "x": "Example that switches from animateTo to an instant jumpTo when the user has enabled system reduced motion."}, {"u": "api/ensure-visible.html", "a": "", "p": "Scrollable.ensureVisible()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Use Scrollable.ensureVisible() to scroll any mounted BuildContext into the visible viewport window."}, {"u": "api/ensure-visible.html", "a": "", "p": "Scrollable.ensureVisible()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Scrollable.ensureVisible walks up every enclosing Scrollable from a target BuildContext and scrolls each one so the target's RenderObject becomes visible inside the viewport. The target widget must currently be mounted (built and laid out in the tree). Respects maxScrollObstructionExtent reported by pinned slivers like PinnedHeaderSliver and SliverAppBar(pinned: true)."}, {"u": "api/ensure-visible.html", "a": "signature-and-defaults", "p": "Scrollable.ensureVisible()", "h": "Signature and Defaults", "k": "API", "x": "With default arguments, ensureVisible jumps immediately (Duration.zero) and aligns the target with the viewport's leading edge (alignment: 0.0):"}, {"u": "api/ensure-visible.html", "a": "center-a-keyed-section-widget-smoothly", "p": "Scrollable.ensureVisible()", "h": "Center a Keyed Section Widget Smoothly", "k": "API", "x": "Example that centers a mounted form error or section header (alignment: 0.5) inside the viewport."}, {"u": "api/ensure-visible.html", "a": "reveal-only-if-clipped-below-the-bottom-edge", "p": "Scrollable.ensureVisible()", "h": "Reveal Only If Clipped Below the Bottom Edge", "k": "API", "x": "Example for keyboard down-arrow selection: leaves the scroll offset untouched if the row is already fully visible, and scrolls just far enough to show its bottom edge if clipped."}, {"u": "api/ensure-visible.html", "a": "reveal-only-if-clipped-above-the-top-edge", "p": "Scrollable.ensureVisible()", "h": "Reveal Only If Clipped Above the Top Edge", "k": "API", "x": "Example for keyboard up-arrow selection: scrolls up just far enough to clear the leading edge (and any pinned header) if the row sits above the visible area."}, {"u": "api/create-scroll-position.html", "a": "", "p": "createScrollPosition()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Override createScrollPosition() in a custom ScrollController to customize position initialization or subclass ScrollPosition."}, {"u": "api/create-scroll-position.html", "a": "", "p": "createScrollPosition()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "The createScrollPosition(ScrollPhysics physics, ScrollContext context, ScrollPosition? oldPosition) method on ScrollController constructs the ScrollPosition owned by a mounting or updating Scrollable."}, {"u": "api/create-scroll-position.html", "a": "default-implementation", "p": "createScrollPosition()", "h": "Default Implementation", "k": "API", "x": "Constructs a standard ScrollPositionWithSingleContext seeded with initialScrollOffset and keepScrollOffset:"}, {"u": "api/create-scroll-position.html", "a": "dynamic-initial-offset-computed-at-mount-time", "p": "createScrollPosition()", "h": "Dynamic Initial Offset Computed at Mount Time", "k": "API", "x": "Example of a ScrollController subclass that computes its initial pixel offset dynamically (for instance from viewport-independent app state) whenever a new Scrollable attaches."}, {"u": "api/create-scroll-position.html", "a": "synchronize-initial-offset-with-a-sibling-controller", "p": "createScrollPosition()", "h": "Synchronize Initial Offset With a Sibling Controller", "k": "API", "x": "Example where a secondary ListView starts at the exact current offset of an already-mounted primary ScrollController when swapped onto screen."}, {"u": "api/scroll-metrics.html", "a": "", "p": "ScrollMetrics", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Read ScrollMetrics properties on ScrollPosition, ScrollNotification, and ScrollPhysics callbacks."}, {"u": "api/scroll-metrics.html", "a": "", "p": "ScrollMetrics", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "ScrollMetrics is the read-only interface describing a scroll view's current offset, bounds, and window size. Both ScrollPosition (live, mutable) and FixedScrollMetrics (the immutable snapshot carried inside ScrollNotification.metrics) implement ScrollMetrics."}, {"u": "api/scroll-metrics.html", "a": "core-fields-and-derived-getters", "p": "ScrollMetrics", "h": "Core Fields and Derived Getters", "k": "API", "x": "pixels double Current scroll offset relative to origin minScrollExtent double Smallest in-range offset (0.0 in standard forward lists) maxScrollExtent double Largest in-range offset (0.0 when all content fits in viewport) viewportDimension double Main-axis length of the viewport window axisDirection / axis AxisDirection / Axis Direction in which pixels increases extentBefore double max(pixels - minScrollExtent, 0.0) extentInside double Visible slice of in-range content inside viewport extentAfter double max(maxScrollExtent - pixels, 0.0) extentTotal double maxScrollExtent - minScrollExtent + viewportDimension outOfRange bool pixels < minScrollExtent || pixels > maxScrollExtent atEdge bool pixels == minScrollExtent || pixels == maxScrollExtent"}, {"u": "api/scroll-metrics.html", "a": "compute-safe-0010-scroll-progress", "p": "ScrollMetrics", "h": "Compute Safe 0.0..1.0 Scroll Progress", "k": "API", "x": "Example that converts ScrollMetrics into a clamped progress fraction without dividing by zero when all content fits on screen."}, {"u": "api/scroll-metrics.html", "a": "detect-near-bottom-pagination-threshold", "p": "ScrollMetrics", "h": "Detect Near-Bottom Pagination Threshold", "k": "API", "x": "Example that checks extentAfter so infinite-scroll prefetching triggers 300px before the user hits the end."}, {"u": "api/scroll-metrics.html", "a": "guard-scrollposition-reads-before-first-layout", "p": "ScrollMetrics", "h": "Guard ScrollPosition Reads Before First Layout", "k": "API", "x": "Example of checking hasContentDimensions and hasPixels on a live ScrollPosition when reading outside a notification callback."}, {"u": "api/on-notification.html", "a": "", "p": "NotificationListener<ScrollNotification>", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Handle bubbling ScrollNotifications and ScrollMetricsNotifications from any descendant scrollable."}, {"u": "api/on-notification.html", "a": "", "p": "NotificationListener<ScrollNotification>", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "NotificationListener<T> intercepts scroll notifications bubbling up the element tree via its bool Function(T notification)? onNotification callback. Return false to allow the notification to keep bubbling to ancestors (Scrollbar, RefreshIndicator, Scaffold). Return true to stop bubbling at this listener."}, {"u": "api/on-notification.html", "a": "default-filter-defaultscrollnotificationpredicate", "p": "NotificationListener<ScrollNotification>", "h": "Default Filter (defaultScrollNotificationPredicate)", "k": "API", "x": "Any nested scroll view (like a horizontal carousel inside a vertical list) bubbles through outer listeners with notification.depth > 0. Filter to the nearest child viewport with notification.depth == 0:"}, {"u": "api/on-notification.html", "a": "show-app-bar-divider-only-when-scrolled-under", "p": "NotificationListener<ScrollNotification>", "h": "Show App-Bar Divider Only When Scrolled Under", "k": "API", "x": "Example that toggles setState only when the boolean isElevated actually flips, avoiding 60fps widget rebuilds."}, {"u": "api/on-notification.html", "a": "hide-floating-action-button-on-downward-drag", "p": "NotificationListener<ScrollNotification>", "h": "Hide Floating Action Button on Downward Drag", "k": "API", "x": "Example that listens specifically to UserScrollNotification so programmatic animations or bounce-back springs do not hide the button unexpectedly."}, {"u": "api/on-notification.html", "a": "react-when-content-grows-without-scrolling-scrollmetricsnotification", "p": "NotificationListener<ScrollNotification>", "h": "React When Content Grows Without Scrolling (ScrollMetricsNotification)", "k": "API", "x": "Example that catches post-layout extent changes (ScrollMetricsNotification does not extend ScrollNotification)."}, {"u": "api/sliver-constraints.html", "a": "", "p": "SliverConstraints", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Read SliverConstraints inside RenderSliver.performLayout() or SliverLayoutBuilder."}, {"u": "api/sliver-constraints.html", "a": "", "p": "SliverConstraints", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "SliverConstraints is the input object handed down by RenderViewport (or an enclosing adapter sliver such as RenderSliverPadding) to a RenderSliver on every layout pass."}, {"u": "api/sliver-constraints.html", "a": "key-properties", "p": "SliverConstraints", "h": "Key Properties", "k": "API", "x": "axisDirection / axis AxisDirection / Axis Main scroll direction (down, up, right, left) growthDirection GrowthDirection forward after center key, reverse before center key userScrollDirection ScrollDirection Most recent user drag direction (forward, reverse, idle) scrollOffset double Pixels of this sliver scrolled past the leading edge (>= 0.0) precedingScrollExtent double Sum of scrollExtent of all earlier slivers (>= 0.0) overlap double Pixels at this sliver's start covered by earlier pinned/floating slivers remainingPaintExtent double Visible viewport space remaining from this sliver's start (>= 0.0) crossAxisExtent double Viewport width (when vertical) or height (when horizontal) viewportMainAxisExtent double Full main-axis length of the viewport window cacheOrigin double Start of cache region relative to scrollOffset (<= 0.0, e.g. -250.0) remainingCacheExtent double Total length from cacheOrigin to trailing cache edge"}, {"u": "api/sliver-constraints.html", "a": "convert-to-boxconstraints-for-a-child-renderbox", "p": "SliverConstraints", "h": "Convert to BoxConstraints for a Child RenderBox", "k": "API", "x": "Example using asBoxConstraints inside a RenderSliver to tighten the cross axis while leaving the main axis unconstrained (or bounded)."}, {"u": "api/sliver-constraints.html", "a": "responsive-sliver-grid-columns-sliverlayoutbuilder", "p": "SliverConstraints", "h": "Responsive Sliver Grid Columns (SliverLayoutBuilder)", "k": "API", "x": "Example of reading SliverConstraints.crossAxisExtent in widget code without writing a custom RenderSliver."}, {"u": "api/sliver-constraints.html", "a": "check-whether-this-sliver-has-reached-the-pinned-edge", "p": "SliverConstraints", "h": "Check Whether This Sliver Has Reached the Pinned Edge", "k": "API", "x": "Example inside performLayout checking whether the leading edge (or an earlier pinned header's bottom edge) has touched this sliver."}, {"u": "api/sliver-geometry.html", "a": "", "p": "SliverGeometry", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Construct a valid SliverGeometry inside RenderSliver.performLayout()."}, {"u": "api/sliver-geometry.html", "a": "", "p": "SliverGeometry", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "SliverGeometry is the immutable answer a RenderSliver writes to this.geometry before performLayout() returns. Every debug build validates geometry with debugAssertIsValid, enforcing layoutExtent <= paintExtent, paintExtent <= maxPaintExtent, and paintOrigin + paintExtent <= constraints.remainingPaintExtent."}, {"u": "api/sliver-geometry.html", "a": "constructor-fields-and-defaults", "p": "SliverGeometry", "h": "Constructor Fields and Defaults", "k": "API", "x": "scrollExtent 0.0 Total scrollable length contributed to maxScrollExtent paintExtent 0.0 Pixels painted inside the viewport right now paintOrigin 0.0 Offset from this sliver's layout start where painting begins layoutExtent paintExtent How far the viewport advances the next sliver's start maxPaintExtent 0.0 Largest extent this sliver could ever paint (>= paintExtent) maxScrollObstructionExtent 0.0 Pixels permanently obstructing content for ensureVisible hitTestExtent paintExtent Main-axis range accepting pointer hits visible paintExtent > 0.0 Whether RenderViewport calls paint() on this sliver hasVisualOverflow false Hint that children extend outside paintExtent and may need clipping scrollOffsetCorrection null Non-zero value aborts layout, adjusts offset by this delta, and retries cacheExtent (calculated) Portion of remainingCacheExtent consumed by this sliver"}, {"u": "api/sliver-geometry.html", "a": "empty--collapsed-sliver-slivergeometryzero", "p": "SliverGeometry", "h": "Empty / Collapsed Sliver (SliverGeometry.zero)", "k": "API", "x": "Example when a sliver has no child or 0.0 extent."}, {"u": "api/sliver-geometry.html", "a": "ordinary-scrolling-sliver", "p": "SliverGeometry", "h": "Ordinary Scrolling Sliver", "k": "API", "x": "Example where layoutExtent equals paintExtent, so the next sliver starts immediately after the visible portion of this sliver."}, {"u": "api/sliver-geometry.html", "a": "pinned-sliver-layoutextent--paintextent", "p": "SliverGeometry", "h": "Pinned Sliver (layoutExtent < paintExtent)", "k": "API", "x": "Example where paintExtent stays full height while layoutExtent shrinks to 0.0, allowing subsequent slivers to slide underneath."}, {"u": "api/sliver-geometry.html", "a": "request-a-scroll-offset-correction-mid-layout", "p": "SliverGeometry", "h": "Request a Scroll Offset Correction Mid-Layout", "k": "API", "x": "Example when a lazy sliver discovers an item above the viewport was taller than previously estimated. When scrollOffsetCorrection is non-null, RenderViewport immediately adjusts ViewportOffset and re-runs layout; no other fields on SliverGeometry need to be populated."}, {"u": "api/sliver-perform-layout.html", "a": "", "p": "performLayout()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Implement performLayout() in a custom RenderSliver or RenderSliverSingleBoxAdapter."}, {"u": "api/sliver-perform-layout.html", "a": "", "p": "performLayout()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "RenderSliver.performLayout() has two responsibilities: Lay out any visible children using this.constraints (SliverConstraints). Assign a valid SliverGeometry to this.geometry before returning."}, {"u": "api/sliver-perform-layout.html", "a": "default-implementation---there-is-none", "p": "performLayout()", "h": "Default Implementation - There Is None", "k": "API", "x": "RenderSliver does not provide a default performLayout(). Every concrete sliver subclass must set geometry on every call — leaving geometry null triggers a framework assertion immediately after performLayout() returns."}, {"u": "api/sliver-perform-layout.html", "a": "childless-spacer-sliver", "p": "performLayout()", "h": "Childless Spacer Sliver", "k": "API", "x": "Example of a sliver with no child that contributes a fixed extent of scrollable blank space (similar to SliverToBoxAdapter(child: SizedBox(height: extent)) without allocating a RenderBox)."}, {"u": "api/sliver-perform-layout.html", "a": "single-child---fill-remaining-viewport-space", "p": "performLayout()", "h": "Single Child - Fill Remaining Viewport Space", "k": "API", "x": "Example where a single RenderBox stretches to fill whatever space remains in the viewport when content above it is short, while never shrinking smaller than its own intrinsic height (the core idea of SliverFillRemaining(hasScrollBody: false))."}, {"u": "api/sliver-perform-layout.html", "a": "single-child---parallax--overscroll-stretch-header", "p": "performLayout()", "h": "Single Child - Parallax / Overscroll Stretch Header", "k": "API", "x": "Example where pulling past the top of a BouncingScrollPhysics viewport (constraints.overlap < 0.0 or precedingScrollExtent == 0.0) stretches the header box to cover the gap."}, {"u": "api/child-main-axis-position.html", "a": "", "p": "childMainAxisPosition()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Implement childMainAxisPosition() in a custom RenderSliver to align painting, hit testing, and coordinate transforms."}, {"u": "api/child-main-axis-position.html", "a": "", "p": "childMainAxisPosition()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "The childMainAxisPosition(covariant RenderObject child) method returns a child's main-axis paint position relative to the sliver's own zero point (constraints.scrollOffset inside the sliver). RenderSliverSingleBoxAdapter and RenderSliverMultiBoxAdaptor read childMainAxisPosition in three places — paint, hitTestBoxChild, and applyPaintTransform — so overriding this one method keeps drawing and pointer hits automatically synchronized."}, {"u": "api/child-main-axis-position.html", "a": "default-implementation", "p": "childMainAxisPosition()", "h": "Default Implementation", "k": "API", "x": "On the base RenderSliver, calling childMainAxisPosition asserts and throws unless overridden:"}, {"u": "api/child-main-axis-position.html", "a": "normal-scrolling-single-box--constraintsscrolloffset", "p": "childMainAxisPosition()", "h": "Normal Scrolling Single Box (-constraints.scrollOffset)", "k": "API", "x": "Example used by RenderSliverToBoxAdapter: as constraints.scrollOffset increases, the child slides off the leading edge by that exact distance (and setChildParentData writes the matching paintOffset onto SliverPhysicalParentData)."}, {"u": "api/child-main-axis-position.html", "a": "pinned-header-single-box-00", "p": "childMainAxisPosition()", "h": "Pinned Header Single Box (0.0)", "k": "API", "x": "Example used by PinnedHeaderSliver and Sticky Header: the child never slides past the sliver's leading edge, remaining anchored at 0.0 (shifted by geometry.paintOrigin if earlier headers overlap)."}, {"u": "api/child-main-axis-position.html", "a": "multi-child-list-item-layoutoffset---constraintsscrolloffset", "p": "childMainAxisPosition()", "h": "Multi-Child List Item (layoutOffset - constraints.scrollOffset)", "k": "API", "x": "Example used by RenderSliverMultiBoxAdaptor (SliverList / SliverGrid): each child stores its content-space start offset in SliverMultiBoxAdaptorParentData.layoutOffset, and subtracting constraints.scrollOffset converts that into viewport-relative coordinates."}, {"u": "api/get-scroll-physics.html", "a": "", "p": "getScrollPhysics()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "Override getScrollPhysics() on ScrollBehavior (or use copyWith) to set default ScrollPhysics for a subtree or app."}, {"u": "api/get-scroll-physics.html", "a": "", "p": "getScrollPhysics()", "h": "", "k": "API", "x": "The getScrollPhysics(BuildContext context) method on ScrollBehavior supplies the ambient ScrollPhysics chain for every Scrollable in a ScrollConfiguration subtree. When a ScrollView passes an explicit physics:, ScrollableState runs widget.physics!.applyTo(scrollBehavior.getScrollPhysics(context)). When physics: is null, ScrollableState uses scrollBehavior.getScrollPhysics(context) directly."}, {"u": "api/get-scroll-physics.html", "a": "default-implementation", "p": "getScrollPhysics()", "h": "Default Implementation", "k": "API", "x": "Base ScrollBehavior switches on getPlatform(context) (defaultTargetPlatform), pairing BouncingScrollPhysics on Apple platforms and ClampingScrollPhysics everywhere else with RangeMaintainingScrollPhysics as the parent:"}, {"u": "api/get-scroll-physics.html", "a": "force-ios-style-bounce-across-all-platforms-copywith", "p": "getScrollPhysics()", "h": "Force iOS-Style Bounce Across All Platforms (copyWith)", "k": "API", "x": "Example that applies BouncingScrollPhysics across Android, desktop, and web in MaterialApp without writing a subclass."}, {"u": "api/get-scroll-physics.html", "a": "subclass-materialscrollbehavior-for-custom-app-wide-physics", "p": "getScrollPhysics()", "h": "Subclass MaterialScrollBehavior for Custom App-Wide Physics", "k": "API", "x": "Example when your physics depend on BuildContext (for instance disabling overscroll bounce when accessibility reduced motion is active)."}, {"u": "api/get-scroll-physics.html", "a": "override-physics-locally-for-one-subtree", "p": "getScrollPhysics()", "h": "Override Physics Locally for One Subtree", "k": "API", "x": "Example wrapping a single screen or panel in ScrollConfiguration while keeping the surrounding app's scrollbars and overscroll indicators intact."}, {"u": "examples/index.html", "a": "", "p": "Examples", "h": "", "k": "Example", "x": "Practical, minimal examples of custom scrolling physics, viewports, and slivers."}, {"u": "examples/index.html", "a": "", "p": "Examples", "h": "", "k": "Example", "x": "Conceptual understanding of scrolling is essential, but it sticks fastest when you can see every line of a working implementation and drag it around yourself. This section walks through the design and implementation decisions of three small, complete scrolling components — one custom ScrollPhysics, one custom Viewport, and one custom RenderSliver. To gain a higher-level understanding of the scrolling pipeline, see the Guides section. For quick recipes on individual methods and types, see the API section."}, {"u": "examples/snapping-physics.html", "a": "", "p": "Snapping Physics", "h": "", "k": "Example", "x": "Custom ScrollPhysics that always settle on an item boundary."}, {"u": "examples/snapping-physics.html", "a": "", "p": "Snapping Physics", "h": "", "k": "Example", "x": "A horizontal strip of cards where dragging feels completely ordinary while your finger is down, and releasing always settles cleanly onto a card boundary — advancing to the next card on a quick flick or snapping back to the nearest one after a slow drag."}, {"u": "examples/snapping-physics.html", "a": "why-custom-scrollphysics", "p": "Snapping Physics", "h": "Why Custom ScrollPhysics?", "k": "Example", "x": "Flutter ships PageScrollPhysics for PageView, where every page is as wide as the viewport (viewportDimension * viewportFraction). As soon as your items are narrower than the viewport — say a strip of 220px cards inside a 400px screen — PageScrollPhysics snaps to viewport multiples instead of card multiples. You could try listening to ScrollEndNotification and calling controller.animateTo(...) after every scroll finishes. That approach fights the framework: the list decelerates under standard friction first, comes to a stop, and then jerks into a second animation. Custom ScrollPhysics replace the ballistic simulation at the moment the finger lifts, so the card glides into place in one continuous spring motion with the user's release velocity preserved."}, {"u": "examples/snapping-physics.html", "a": "try-it-out", "p": "Snapping Physics", "h": "Try it Out", "k": "Example", "x": "Drag the strip left or right, flick it quickly, or switch between SnapScrollPhysics and plain clamping physics to feel the difference when you let go."}, {"u": "examples/snapping-physics.html", "a": "preserving-the-physics-chain-with-applyto", "p": "Snapping Physics", "h": "Preserving the Physics Chain with applyTo", "k": "Example", "x": "Scrollable never uses your ScrollPhysics object directly. It merges the widget's physics with the ambient ScrollBehavior physics by calling physics.applyTo(ambientPhysics). The base ScrollPhysics.applyTo returns a plain ScrollPhysics, so if you forget to override applyTo, your subclass is silently discarded before the first frame. buildParent(ancestor) chains ancestor onto any existing parent already attached to your instance. Overriding shouldUpdate tells ScrollableState to rebuild the merged chain if the caller rebuilds with a different itemExtent."}, {"u": "examples/snapping-physics.html", "a": "choosing-the-landing-offset", "p": "Snapping Physics", "h": "Choosing the Landing Offset", "k": "Example", "x": "When the finger lifts, pixels / itemExtent gives the fractional item index currently aligned with the leading edge. A slow release rounds to the nearest integer. A flick whose speed exceeds tolerance.velocity nudges that fractional index by 0.5 in the fling direction before rounding, which guarantees advancing to the next card even if the finger barely crossed 10% of the card width. Clamping to [minScrollExtent, maxScrollExtent] ensures the last card never snaps past the end of the content when the viewport holds multiple cards at once."}, {"u": "examples/snapping-physics.html", "a": "returning-the-ballistic-simulation", "p": "Snapping Physics", "h": "Returning the Ballistic Simulation", "k": "Example", "x": "createBallisticSimulation runs whenever the position enters a ballistic phase — right after a drag ends, after content dimensions change, or at the end of jumpTo. Two edge cases keep the physics feeling native: If the user is already overscrolled at either end and moving away from the list, delegate to super.createBallisticSimulation so the platform's own bounce or clamp handles the edge. If target == position.pixels, return null so the position transitions straight to IdleScrollActivity without scheduling a zero-distance animation controller tick. Passing the user's release velocity into ScrollSpringSimulation is what makes a fast flick arrive briskly without a visible hitch at liftoff."}, {"u": "examples/snapping-physics.html", "a": "similar-implementations", "p": "Snapping Physics", "h": "Similar Implementations", "k": "Example", "x": "PageScrollPhysics in package:flutter/src/widgets/page_view.dart uses the exact same ±0.5 velocity bias, scaled by position.viewportDimension * viewportFraction instead of a fixed itemExtent. FixedExtentScrollPhysics in package:flutter/src/widgets/list_wheel_scroll_view.dart snaps cylindrical wheel pickers (CupertinoPicker) to integer item indices."}, {"u": "examples/minimal-viewport.html", "a": "", "p": "Minimal Viewport", "h": "", "k": "Example", "x": "Custom RenderBox viewport driven directly by Scrollable and ViewportOffset."}, {"u": "examples/minimal-viewport.html", "a": "", "p": "Minimal Viewport", "h": "", "k": "Example", "x": "A complete vertical scroll view built from Scrollable and a single custom RenderBox (RenderBareViewport). It lays out one tall child, reports the viewport and content dimensions to the ViewportOffset, shifts the child by -offset.pixels at paint time, clips to its own bounds, and translates pointer hits to match."}, {"u": "examples/minimal-viewport.html", "a": "why-build-a-bare-viewport", "p": "Minimal Viewport", "h": "Why Build a Bare Viewport?", "k": "Example", "x": "Between ListView, CustomScrollView, RenderViewport, and a dozen sliver classes, it is easy to assume scrolling is deeply wired into slivers. Building a working scroll view without a single sliver strips away all of that machinery and shows the exact contract between Scrollable and whatever renders the content. It is also the real architecture behind SingleChildScrollView, which uses a private _RenderSingleChildViewport rather than a sliver Viewport when all you have is one RenderBox child that fits comfortably in memory."}, {"u": "examples/minimal-viewport.html", "a": "try-it-out", "p": "Minimal Viewport", "h": "Try it Out", "k": "Example", "x": "Drag the list up and down, click any visible item to test hit-testing through the scroll offset, and toggle ClipRect off to peek at how the full child is positioned behind the window."}, {"u": "examples/minimal-viewport.html", "a": "connecting-scrollable-to-the-viewport", "p": "Minimal Viewport", "h": "Connecting Scrollable to the Viewport", "k": "Example", "x": "Scrollable handles drag gestures, wheel events, physics, ballistic flings, scrollbars, and overscroll indicators. All it asks from you is a viewportBuilder callback that accepts a ViewportOffset and returns the widget that paints the window. Mixing ViewportElementMixin into BareViewport's element increments ScrollNotification.depth as notifications bubble past this viewport, matching the behavior of Flutter's built-in viewports when scroll views are nested."}, {"u": "examples/minimal-viewport.html", "a": "listening-to-viewportoffset-for-repaints-only", "p": "Minimal Viewport", "h": "Listening to ViewportOffset for Repaints Only", "k": "Example", "x": "Because RenderBareViewport lays out its single child in full up front, scrolling never changes the child's size or layout. Attaching markNeedsPaint (not markNeedsLayout) as the listener on offset means every scroll frame skips layout completely and only repaints inside this render object's own repaint boundary."}, {"u": "examples/minimal-viewport.html", "a": "reporting-dimensions-in-performlayout", "p": "Minimal Viewport", "h": "Reporting Dimensions in performLayout", "k": "Example", "x": "During performLayout, the child gets the parent's width constraints and an unbounded height (constraints.widthConstraints()), then RenderBareViewport constrains its own size to the incoming box constraints. Before returning, it fulfills the entire ViewportOffset contract in two calls: applyViewportDimension and applyContentDimensions. If the child shrank while scrolled near the bottom, offset.correctBy(...) silently pulls pixels back into range without notifying listeners mid-layout."}, {"u": "examples/minimal-viewport.html", "a": "painting-transforms-and-hit-testing", "p": "Minimal Viewport", "h": "Painting, Transforms, and Hit Testing", "k": "Example", "x": "When offset.pixels is +150.0, the user has scrolled 150px down into the content, so the child must be painted 150px upward at Offset(0.0, -offset.pixels). Three methods must agree on that exact _paintOffset: paint, applyPaintTransform, and hitTestChildren. Reusing _clipRectLayer.layer via LayerHandle<ClipRectLayer> avoids allocating a fresh engine clip layer on every frame, and result.addWithPaintOffset subtracts _paintOffset from incoming tap coordinates so taps hit the row actually drawn under the finger."}, {"u": "examples/minimal-viewport.html", "a": "similar-implementations", "p": "Minimal Viewport", "h": "Similar Implementations", "k": "Example", "x": "_RenderSingleChildViewport in package:flutter/src/widgets/single_child_scroll_view.dart adds horizontal axis directions, reverse, clipBehavior, and RenderAbstractViewport reveal support on top of this exact skeleton. RenderListWheelViewport in package:flutter/src/rendering/list_wheel_viewport.dart connects to ViewportOffset the same way while painting children wrapped around a 3D cylinder instead of a flat plane."}, {"u": "examples/sticky-header.html", "a": "", "p": "Sticky Header Sliver", "h": "", "k": "Example", "x": "Custom RenderSliver that scrolls to the leading edge and pins in place."}, {"u": "examples/sticky-header.html", "a": "", "p": "Sticky Header Sliver", "h": "", "k": "Example", "x": "A custom sliver (StickyHeader + RenderStickyHeader) that scrolls upward with normal content until its top edge reaches the top of the viewport, then stays pinned there while the SliverList after it slides smoothly underneath."}, {"u": "examples/sticky-header.html", "a": "why-write-a-sticky-header-sliver", "p": "Sticky Header Sliver", "h": "Why Write a Sticky Header Sliver?", "k": "Example", "x": "Flutter ships PinnedHeaderSliver out of the box, so in production code you rarely need to write this class yourself. Writing it once is the fastest way to make the sliver protocol click: almost every confusing field on SliverConstraints and SliverGeometry — scrollOffset, overlap, scrollExtent, paintExtent, layoutExtent, and childMainAxisPosition — has one clear, visible job here."}, {"u": "examples/sticky-header.html", "a": "try-it-out", "p": "Sticky Header Sliver", "h": "Try it Out", "k": "Example", "x": "Drag the list or jump to the preset offsets and watch geometry.layoutExtent shrink from 44.0 down to 0.0 while geometry.paintExtent stays 44.0."}, {"u": "examples/sticky-header.html", "a": "extending-rendersliversingleboxadapter", "p": "Sticky Header Sliver", "h": "Extending RenderSliverSingleBoxAdapter", "k": "Example", "x": "A sliver cannot host a RenderBox child without a bridge between SliverConstraints and BoxConstraints. Extending RenderSliverSingleBoxAdapter gives you single-child management, painting, hit-testing, and applyPaintTransform for free — leaving just two methods to implement: childMainAxisPosition and performLayout. In a normal SliverToBoxAdapter, childMainAxisPosition returns -constraints.scrollOffset so the child slides out of view as scrollOffset grows. Returning 0.0 keeps the child anchored to the sliver's own origin at all times."}, {"u": "examples/sticky-header.html", "a": "splitting-layoutextent-from-paintextent", "p": "Sticky Header Sliver", "h": "Splitting layoutExtent from paintExtent", "k": "Example", "x": "Before the header reaches the top of the viewport, constraints.scrollOffset is 0.0: earlier slivers absorbed the scroll offset, and the viewport placed this sliver right below them. Once the earlier slivers have scrolled completely off-screen, constraints.scrollOffset starts climbing from 0.0 upward. That is the moment layoutExtent and paintExtent diverge: paintExtent stays equal to childExtent (say 56.0), because the header is still drawn in full at the top of the viewport. layoutExtent shrinks as childExtent - constraints.scrollOffset, clamped to 0.0. RenderViewport advances the next sliver's starting position by layoutExtent, not paintExtent — so as layoutExtent shrinks to zero, the SliverList moves up underneath the still-painted header."}, {"u": "examples/sticky-header.html", "a": "playing-nicely-with-other-slivers", "p": "Sticky Header Sliver", "h": "Playing Nicely With Other Slivers", "k": "Example", "x": "Three smaller fields on SliverGeometry make the header cooperate with sibling slivers and Scrollable.ensureVisible: paintOrigin: constraints.overlap shifts this header after any earlier pinned sliver so two consecutive sticky headers stack cleanly instead of painting on top of one another. maxScrollObstructionExtent: childExtent tells RenderViewport.getOffsetToReveal that the top 56px of the viewport is permanently covered, so ensureVisible scrolls target items below the pinned header rather than hiding them behind it. hasVisualOverflow: true lets RenderSliverSingleBoxAdapter know the sliver paints past its layoutExtent."}, {"u": "examples/sticky-header.html", "a": "similar-implementations", "p": "Sticky Header Sliver", "h": "Similar Implementations", "k": "Example", "x": "_RenderPinnedHeaderSliver in package:flutter/src/widgets/pinned_header_sliver.dart is the framework's production version of this exact sliver, adding semantics scrolling exclusion and explicit clip handling. RenderSliverPinnedPersistentHeader in package:flutter/src/rendering/sliver_persistent_header.dart adds shrinking between maxExtent and minExtent on top of the same layoutExtent vs paintExtent split."}];
